00:11:16.706 --> 00:11:19.250
- [Explainer] What is Approval Voting?

00:11:19.250 --> 00:11:23.070
Simply put, it's a better
way to run an election.

00:11:23.070 --> 00:11:25.250
Let's take a trip to Plantsville.

00:11:25.250 --> 00:11:28.710
It's election time, and Mayor
Blueberry is campaigning hard

00:11:28.710 --> 00:11:30.760
for a second term in office.

00:11:30.760 --> 00:11:34.600
She won the last election
with 65% against her opponent

00:11:34.600 --> 00:11:37.833
Mr. Squash, and she still
enjoys strong support.

00:11:38.670 --> 00:11:42.260
Once again, Mr. Squash is quick
to challenge Ms. Blueberry,

00:11:42.260 --> 00:11:43.810
but this time they're joined

00:11:43.810 --> 00:11:46.200
by a third challenger, Mr. Peach,

00:11:46.200 --> 00:11:48.703
who shared similar views
with mayor Blueberry.

00:11:49.570 --> 00:11:53.370
Mr. Peach, sweet talks almost
half of Blueberry supporters

00:11:53.370 --> 00:11:55.480
into switching their vote to him

00:11:55.480 --> 00:12:00.010
while Mr. Squash holds the
same 35% he had last time.

00:12:00.010 --> 00:12:02.790
The votes are counted, and what's this?

00:12:02.790 --> 00:12:04.550
Mr. Squash wins.

00:12:04.550 --> 00:12:07.340
Blueberry and Peach have
split the fruit vote.

00:12:07.340 --> 00:12:09.140
How did this happen?

00:12:09.140 --> 00:12:11.760
Peach's supporters also like Blueberry,

00:12:11.760 --> 00:12:14.000
but couldn't say so on their ballots.

00:12:14.000 --> 00:12:18.270
A simple solution is to change
the ballot from vote for one

00:12:18.270 --> 00:12:21.220
to vote for one or more, allowing everyone

00:12:21.220 --> 00:12:23.950
to state all the candidates they support.

00:12:23.950 --> 00:12:26.083
This is called Approval Voting.

00:12:27.060 --> 00:12:28.270
With Approval Voting,

00:12:28.270 --> 00:12:31.180
the election would have
gone quite differently.

00:12:31.180 --> 00:12:33.440
Peach's supporters, no longer fear that

00:12:33.440 --> 00:12:36.250
a vote for Peach will help elect Squash.

00:12:36.250 --> 00:12:38.580
Instead, they show their sincere support

00:12:38.580 --> 00:12:41.010
for Peach and also Blueberry.

00:12:41.010 --> 00:12:44.680
They wanna prevent Mr. Squash
from winning and they do.

00:12:44.680 --> 00:12:48.520
An Approval Voting accurately
reflects Peach's support.

00:12:48.520 --> 00:12:51.100
Mayor Blueberry wins the election,

00:12:51.100 --> 00:12:54.000
democracy is restored.

00:12:54.000 --> 00:12:56.490
Approval Voting is more
than just a smart idea

00:12:56.490 --> 00:13:00.960
in Plantsville, it's a smart
idea anywhere you vote.

00:13:00.960 --> 00:13:04.770
Approval Voting is used by
organizations across the globe,

00:13:04.770 --> 00:13:06.450
and for good reason.

00:13:06.450 --> 00:13:08.470
It's democratic because the candidate

00:13:08.470 --> 00:13:10.470
with the most support wins.

00:13:10.470 --> 00:13:13.000
It removes the spoiler effect.

00:13:13.000 --> 00:13:14.470
Even losing candidates

00:13:14.470 --> 00:13:17.240
get an accurate reflection of support,

00:13:17.240 --> 00:13:19.903
and voting your favorite never hurts you.

00:13:20.770 --> 00:13:23.250
Start the conversation on Approval Voting

00:13:23.250 --> 00:13:24.830
and share this video.

00:13:24.830 --> 00:13:27.870
Then join the Center
for Election Science at

00:13:27.870 --> 00:13:29.680
electrology.org.

00:13:29.680 --> 00:13:31.873
Better voting starts with you.

00:13:36.520 --> 00:13:37.790
- Thank you.

00:13:37.790 --> 00:13:41.350
And Approval Voting really
is the simple solution

00:13:41.350 --> 00:13:44.050
that we need to upgrade our democracy.

00:13:44.050 --> 00:13:46.460
And we don't need
establishment gatekeepers

00:13:46.460 --> 00:13:49.010
to tell us what we can and can't do.

00:13:49.010 --> 00:13:52.320
Our democracy and wellbeing
is just too important

00:13:52.320 --> 00:13:54.230
to have to wait for permission.

00:13:54.230 --> 00:13:57.240
Indeed, in Fargo and St. Louis,

00:13:57.240 --> 00:14:00.010
the establishment there
was not very excited

00:14:00.010 --> 00:14:02.850
about Approval voting and
that's because they knew

00:14:02.850 --> 00:14:04.760
it was going to challenge their power.

00:14:04.760 --> 00:14:08.460
And so, when we worked
with key stakeholders

00:14:08.460 --> 00:14:11.880
within that community, we worked with them

00:14:11.880 --> 00:14:14.090
to help bring them that power back.

00:14:14.090 --> 00:14:16.090
And interestingly, the two places

00:14:16.090 --> 00:14:19.810
where we've worked so
far, Fargo and St. Louis,

00:14:19.810 --> 00:14:21.620
I think on the surface it appears like

00:14:21.620 --> 00:14:23.670
these are really different places,

00:14:23.670 --> 00:14:27.490
but they were looking at
really three core components

00:14:27.490 --> 00:14:28.690
of a voting method.

00:14:28.690 --> 00:14:30.030
And these are components that we're gonna

00:14:30.030 --> 00:14:31.560
keep coming back to.

00:14:31.560 --> 00:14:33.370
One is, you want a voting method

00:14:33.370 --> 00:14:36.980
that is robust to vote splitting,

00:14:36.980 --> 00:14:40.910
is able to show a clear
mandate when one is present,

00:14:40.910 --> 00:14:44.150
and being able to like
to consistent candidate.

00:14:44.150 --> 00:14:46.490
Second is, being able to have an idea

00:14:46.490 --> 00:14:49.352
of how much support
candidates actually have.

00:14:49.352 --> 00:14:51.470
You wanna make sure that voices are heard.

00:14:51.470 --> 00:14:53.923
And if you're not able to show the support

00:14:53.923 --> 00:14:56.600
of all the candidates,
ell, you're missing out

00:14:56.600 --> 00:14:58.720
on all of these different voices.

00:14:58.720 --> 00:15:02.750
And thirdly, you want a
voting method that is not

00:15:02.750 --> 00:15:06.100
unnecessarily expensive
and not overly complicated.

00:15:06.100 --> 00:15:08.840
With the votes putting
in mandate component,

00:15:08.840 --> 00:15:12.690
you had candidates winning
with just a mere fraction

00:15:12.690 --> 00:15:14.960
of the vote in both Fargo and St. Louis.

00:15:14.960 --> 00:15:16.920
Since Approval Voting
has been implemented,

00:15:16.920 --> 00:15:20.215
we've seen candidates be winning with,

00:15:20.215 --> 00:15:24.893
or leading with well over a 50% support.

00:15:26.250 --> 00:15:29.180
And it's not to say that that
will always happen again.

00:15:29.180 --> 00:15:32.070
No voting method can guarantee a majority.

00:15:32.070 --> 00:15:34.610
So, like when we see Rank Choice Voting

00:15:34.610 --> 00:15:37.040
advocates talk about
always getting a majority,

00:15:37.040 --> 00:15:39.743
that's really an artificial majority.

00:15:41.170 --> 00:15:43.520
If you throw away all
the candidates but two,

00:15:43.520 --> 00:15:45.180
of course you're gonna get a majority,

00:15:45.180 --> 00:15:46.990
any voting method can do that.

00:15:46.990 --> 00:15:48.750
And we're gonna talk again and again,

00:15:48.750 --> 00:15:50.210
about being able to share with reflection,

00:15:50.210 --> 00:15:51.420
support for candidates.

00:15:51.420 --> 00:15:55.180
We're gonna talk about polling,
and how that's played out

00:15:55.180 --> 00:15:58.050
in terms of Approval Voting,
showing the accurate reflection

00:15:58.050 --> 00:16:00.360
of support for candidates.

00:16:00.360 --> 00:16:02.397
And both St. Louis and Fargo,

00:16:02.397 --> 00:16:04.850
they were told that
their only choice before

00:16:04.850 --> 00:16:06.490
was Rank Choice Voting, and so,

00:16:06.490 --> 00:16:09.080
they couldn't afford
a new voting machines.

00:16:09.080 --> 00:16:13.510
And so, Approval Voting was
really their only way forward.

00:16:13.510 --> 00:16:15.800
So, we needed an approach
that gives winners

00:16:15.800 --> 00:16:20.459
the necessary support to be
able to make bold decisions,

00:16:20.459 --> 00:16:23.117
we need an approach that
encourages new ideas to flourish,

00:16:23.117 --> 00:16:25.230
and we needed an approach
that is practical.

00:16:25.230 --> 00:16:28.183
So, overall, we need Approval Voting.

00:16:30.540 --> 00:16:32.010
- Thanks Aaron.

00:16:32.010 --> 00:16:34.560
So, for the next 25 to 30 minutes,

00:16:34.560 --> 00:16:36.500
we're gonna go through some questions,

00:16:36.500 --> 00:16:41.500
and each person will have
two to three minutes total;

00:16:43.890 --> 00:16:47.050
two minutes to answer,
save a minute for rebuttal,

00:16:47.050 --> 00:16:49.610
and we'll start with Lee.

00:16:49.610 --> 00:16:52.773
Why is your method
preferable to the other?

00:16:54.300 --> 00:16:55.863
- Great, thank you.

00:16:57.120 --> 00:17:00.550
I think, I mean, the easiest way

00:17:00.550 --> 00:17:05.550
to think about Rank Choice
Voting verse Approval Voting,

00:17:07.130 --> 00:17:12.130
is to think about fact
that one voting system

00:17:13.220 --> 00:17:17.690
has a hundred years of support.

00:17:17.690 --> 00:17:19.860
We know, the evidence is out there,

00:17:19.860 --> 00:17:23.870
Rank Choice Voting works,
whereas Approval Voting,

00:17:23.870 --> 00:17:25.110
it's not a new voting system.

00:17:25.110 --> 00:17:28.030
In fact, it's a voting system

00:17:28.030 --> 00:17:32.170
that is older than Rank Choice Voting.

00:17:32.170 --> 00:17:33.810
So, I think one question that,

00:17:33.810 --> 00:17:38.760
and I'm curious for
Aaron answer to this is,

00:17:38.760 --> 00:17:40.910
why is it that, although Approval Voting

00:17:40.910 --> 00:17:42.870
has been around for a long time,

00:17:42.870 --> 00:17:45.330
it's never really caught on?

00:17:45.330 --> 00:17:47.140
And I mean, I think that the history shows

00:17:47.140 --> 00:17:48.210
that there'd been various moments

00:17:48.210 --> 00:17:50.857
in which some organizations
have been have used it,

00:17:50.857 --> 00:17:52.820
and then they've discarded it

00:17:52.820 --> 00:17:54.800
because it just leads to Bullet voting.

00:17:54.800 --> 00:17:56.900
There've been some other
systems that are similar

00:17:56.900 --> 00:18:00.600
to it that have been discarded.

00:18:00.600 --> 00:18:04.010
And I just think, for elections,

00:18:04.010 --> 00:18:07.220
it just doesn't make a lot of sense.

00:18:07.220 --> 00:18:09.810
Whereas we know that Rank
Choice voting makes sense

00:18:09.810 --> 00:18:10.820
because we know it works,

00:18:10.820 --> 00:18:14.400
because it thinks about
how voters and candidates

00:18:14.400 --> 00:18:17.190
actually campaign and vote.

00:18:17.190 --> 00:18:18.213
Now, I do think that,

00:18:18.213 --> 00:18:21.230
I don't wanna be inherently
dismissive of Approval Voting,

00:18:21.230 --> 00:18:23.350
'cause I think that
there are cases in which

00:18:23.350 --> 00:18:25.840
a system of Approval Voting makes sense,

00:18:25.840 --> 00:18:28.780
and I think there are those
systems where decisions,

00:18:28.780 --> 00:18:29.670
where the stakes are low.

00:18:29.670 --> 00:18:32.340
For example, if you filled
out one of those doodle polls

00:18:32.340 --> 00:18:33.540
where you're trying to figure out

00:18:33.540 --> 00:18:36.450
when a good time to meet is,
well that's Approval Voting

00:18:36.450 --> 00:18:38.450
and that's the value of Approval Voting.

00:18:38.450 --> 00:18:40.888
And it works because
under those conditions,

00:18:40.888 --> 00:18:43.360
most people don't have
really strong preferences.

00:18:43.360 --> 00:18:46.780
They're equivocal, whether it's
we meet on Tuesday at four,

00:18:46.780 --> 00:18:49.685
Wednesday at 11, and everybody's
trying to find a time

00:18:49.685 --> 00:18:51.160
that they can all meet together.

00:18:51.160 --> 00:18:53.940
So, everybody's sincere,
they're not strategic,

00:18:53.940 --> 00:18:55.320
so pretty low stakes thing.

00:18:55.320 --> 00:18:57.620
But if the stakes were a little higher,

00:18:57.620 --> 00:18:59.570
like, say it wasn't just for one meeting

00:18:59.570 --> 00:19:00.960
but a weekly staff meeting,

00:19:00.960 --> 00:19:04.300
well, maybe I think
differently about voting.

00:19:04.300 --> 00:19:07.790
Something about me is that I
like to work in the morning,

00:19:07.790 --> 00:19:09.250
because that's my most productive time.

00:19:09.250 --> 00:19:11.401
I'd rather have meetings in the afternoon,

00:19:11.401 --> 00:19:13.330
I'm more productive earlier in the week.

00:19:13.330 --> 00:19:14.800
I'd rather have meetings Friday,

00:19:14.800 --> 00:19:17.680
so like I'd say like Friday at 3,

00:19:17.680 --> 00:19:20.350
would be my best time for a meeting,

00:19:20.350 --> 00:19:23.340
and that, it might not be a time

00:19:23.340 --> 00:19:25.900
that's good for other people,
but like the question is,

00:19:25.900 --> 00:19:30.730
how can we find the time
that's maybe most optimal

00:19:30.730 --> 00:19:34.030
for all people, assuming
that we have preferences?

00:19:34.030 --> 00:19:36.950
And I think that that's
the fundamental problem

00:19:36.950 --> 00:19:40.450
of Approval Voting, is
that it assumes that

00:19:40.450 --> 00:19:43.560
people are equivocal and
they don't see any difference

00:19:43.560 --> 00:19:45.540
between a bunch of different candidates.

00:19:45.540 --> 00:19:47.548
And the reality is that,

00:19:47.548 --> 00:19:50.240
when it comes to high stakes selections,

00:19:50.240 --> 00:19:54.160
which are elections for
mayor, Senator, governor,

00:19:54.160 --> 00:19:56.670
you sort of keep Single Winner elections,

00:19:56.670 --> 00:19:57.830
people have preferences.

00:19:57.830 --> 00:19:59.270
There are campaigns.

00:19:59.270 --> 00:20:04.270
So, I think, curious why folks
who support Approval Voting

00:20:06.260 --> 00:20:08.914
think that the election
should follow the same logic

00:20:08.914 --> 00:20:12.200
as finding a meeting time on doodle.

00:20:12.200 --> 00:20:16.030
And what are all the people who
recommend Rank Choice Voting

00:20:16.030 --> 00:20:18.060
and have recommended around the world,

00:20:18.060 --> 00:20:19.930
and all the countries and cities

00:20:19.930 --> 00:20:22.395
that have chosen Rank Choice Voting,

00:20:22.395 --> 00:20:25.160
compared to just a very few
have chosen Approval Voting,

00:20:25.160 --> 00:20:26.823
what are they all missing?

00:20:29.070 --> 00:20:31.465
So I think those are
some important questions

00:20:31.465 --> 00:20:33.513
that I hope we can get to here.

00:20:34.650 --> 00:20:38.610
- Aaron, why is your method
preferable to the other?

00:20:38.610 --> 00:20:42.190
- And do you want me
to include my rebuttal

00:20:42.190 --> 00:20:43.600
in my response as well?

00:20:43.600 --> 00:20:44.433
- Absolutely.

00:20:45.670 --> 00:20:46.543
Go for it.

00:20:47.574 --> 00:20:49.410
- So, I'll use the three minutes then.

00:20:49.410 --> 00:20:50.243
- Yeah.

00:20:50.243 --> 00:20:51.810
- Okay.

00:20:51.810 --> 00:20:54.940
So, in terms of why Approval Voting

00:20:54.940 --> 00:20:56.560
is better than my Rank Choice,

00:20:56.560 --> 00:20:59.200
I think, we, again, get
back to those three issues

00:20:59.200 --> 00:21:01.071
being able to elect a strong winner.

00:21:01.071 --> 00:21:04.500
And again, like when we're
talking about a winner,

00:21:04.500 --> 00:21:07.680
one thing to keep in mind is
that nine out of ten times

00:21:07.680 --> 00:21:09.840
Approval Voting and Rank Choice Voting

00:21:09.840 --> 00:21:12.190
are gonna select the same winner.

00:21:12.190 --> 00:21:15.030
I think that's something
that's not really talked

00:21:15.030 --> 00:21:16.010
about a lot.

00:21:16.010 --> 00:21:17.100
And we've seen this play out

00:21:17.100 --> 00:21:19.910
through polling that we've
done in presidential elections,

00:21:19.910 --> 00:21:23.020
Democratic primary, and another elections.

00:21:23.020 --> 00:21:27.810
What really comes to, is
where the focus is in terms of

00:21:27.810 --> 00:21:30.710
where the methods diverge.

00:21:30.710 --> 00:21:35.710
And so, we see one example
here of potential divergence.

00:21:36.010 --> 00:21:37.130
We don't know how Approval
Voting necessarily

00:21:37.130 --> 00:21:39.990
would have played out,
but we see an example

00:21:39.990 --> 00:21:42.220
of where Rank Choice Voting has failed.

00:21:42.220 --> 00:21:44.690
And I think the clear example there

00:21:44.690 --> 00:21:47.660
that no one wants to talk
about is Burlington, Vermont.

00:21:47.660 --> 00:21:51.830
And that's an example where the person

00:21:51.830 --> 00:21:54.590
who was able to beat
everyone else head to head,

00:21:54.590 --> 00:21:58.160
that candidate lost
because of votes splitting

00:21:58.160 --> 00:21:59.690
within Rank Choice Voting.

00:21:59.690 --> 00:22:02.990
There was a vote spitting
with first choice preferences,

00:22:02.990 --> 00:22:06.490
and that candidate was
knocked out and lost.

00:22:06.490 --> 00:22:10.050
And you also hear a lot of
claims that Rank Choice Voting

00:22:10.050 --> 00:22:12.320
lets you always rank
your favorite as first.

00:22:12.320 --> 00:22:16.540
Well, not the case in Burlington, Vermont.

00:22:16.540 --> 00:22:19.880
Conservatives, they were
told the same thing,

00:22:19.880 --> 00:22:22.010
they were told that they could
break their favorite first.

00:22:22.010 --> 00:22:23.100
What happened?

00:22:23.100 --> 00:22:27.810
As a consequence, they
caused a worst outcome.

00:22:27.810 --> 00:22:29.700
They had the progressive candidate,

00:22:29.700 --> 00:22:33.660
the polar opposite of what they
would want win as a result.

00:22:33.660 --> 00:22:35.560
So, it was a worse result for them,

00:22:35.560 --> 00:22:37.810
and it was a worst result
for the electorate.

00:22:38.796 --> 00:22:39.629
And that's because,

00:22:39.629 --> 00:22:42.410
that's even in the case
of voters voting honestly.

00:22:42.410 --> 00:22:43.980
They're getting a worse outcome

00:22:43.980 --> 00:22:46.183
when they're voting honestly.

00:22:47.300 --> 00:22:51.100
And another key example
of being able to see

00:22:51.100 --> 00:22:55.210
when a voting method is performing well,

00:22:55.210 --> 00:22:57.770
it's when it's actually
able to capture the support

00:22:57.770 --> 00:22:58.816
of what people want,

00:22:58.816 --> 00:23:01.800
including with candidates who don't win.

00:23:01.800 --> 00:23:04.483
We see time and again, Approval Voting

00:23:04.483 --> 00:23:06.650
does an excellent job
capturing the support

00:23:06.650 --> 00:23:07.483
of all the candidates,

00:23:07.483 --> 00:23:09.570
including third parties and independence.

00:23:09.570 --> 00:23:12.460
We saw this in the Democratic
primary, for instance.

00:23:12.460 --> 00:23:14.690
The Yang is a great example here.

00:23:14.690 --> 00:23:18.590
Under both Plurality voting
and Rank Choice Voting,

00:23:18.590 --> 00:23:20.360
Yang got under 10%,

00:23:20.360 --> 00:23:23.890
whereas under Approval
Voting, he got roughly 30%.

00:23:23.890 --> 00:23:25.800
This is a case where a candidate

00:23:25.800 --> 00:23:27.860
with new ideas that needs to be heard,

00:23:27.860 --> 00:23:31.060
when people support him
and he's getting ignored.

00:23:31.060 --> 00:23:32.130
Had we used Approval Voting,

00:23:32.130 --> 00:23:34.290
he would have gotten the
support that he deserves.

00:23:34.290 --> 00:23:35.830
And we did a control measure.

00:23:35.830 --> 00:23:38.310
We asked people how they
honestly thought there,

00:23:38.310 --> 00:23:41.910
and we saw that lineup.

00:23:41.910 --> 00:23:45.060
So, it wasn't just a fluke
and we're just seeing,

00:23:45.060 --> 00:23:49.230
time and again, of these
cans getting ignored.

00:23:49.230 --> 00:23:50.800
This idea of momentum,

00:23:50.800 --> 00:23:54.130
I think is one that
doesn't make much sense.

00:23:54.130 --> 00:23:58.560
So, sure, Rank Choice voting
has been around a while

00:23:58.560 --> 00:24:00.270
but like it's also been repealed.

00:24:00.270 --> 00:24:02.270
It's been repealed in Burlington, Vermont,

00:24:02.270 --> 00:24:05.100
Ann Arbor Michigan, Cary, North Carolina,

00:24:05.100 --> 00:24:06.100
Aspen, Colorado,

00:24:06.100 --> 00:24:07.450
Pierce County, Washington,

00:24:07.450 --> 00:24:10.167
Sunnydale, California,
it's failed by over 16%

00:24:10.167 --> 00:24:11.803
and a number of initiatives.

00:24:12.911 --> 00:24:15.880
And Alaska, like it passed recently,

00:24:15.880 --> 00:24:17.840
but it failed the first time,

00:24:17.840 --> 00:24:21.410
Massachusetts, it failed by over a 10%.

00:24:21.410 --> 00:24:23.350
And we can't just celebrate merely

00:24:23.350 --> 00:24:25.780
because a voting method gets a result.

00:24:25.780 --> 00:24:27.240
Any voting method can get a result,

00:24:27.240 --> 00:24:29.110
what we want is a consistent result

00:24:29.110 --> 00:24:30.490
that gives us good winners,

00:24:30.490 --> 00:24:32.530
and a reflection of support
for all the candidates

00:24:32.530 --> 00:24:34.960
and Approval Voting is what does that.

00:24:34.960 --> 00:24:36.080
- Okay.

00:24:36.080 --> 00:24:37.310
Thank you.

00:24:37.310 --> 00:24:42.310
Our next question is about
resistance to strategic voting,

00:24:42.350 --> 00:24:44.875
but what I wanted to do very quickly,

00:24:44.875 --> 00:24:48.660
because everyone might not be
the voting geeks that we are,

00:24:48.660 --> 00:24:51.510
I'll say what strategic voting is.

00:24:51.510 --> 00:24:53.220
Strategic voting, it's sometimes referred

00:24:53.220 --> 00:24:54.730
to as tactical voting.

00:24:54.730 --> 00:24:57.560
It's when a voter cast
their ballot for a candidate

00:24:57.560 --> 00:24:59.610
that is not their first choice,

00:24:59.610 --> 00:25:02.960
in order to prevent
someone else from winning.

00:25:02.960 --> 00:25:07.960
And so, we'll proceed into
the next question, which is,

00:25:09.630 --> 00:25:11.930
which method is more resistant

00:25:11.930 --> 00:25:14.953
to strategic voting and why?

00:25:16.120 --> 00:25:18.070
- Lisa, do you wanna give Lee a chance

00:25:18.070 --> 00:25:19.473
for his rebuttal as well?

00:25:20.340 --> 00:25:21.173
- Okay, Lee.

00:25:21.173 --> 00:25:24.223
- I just wanna be fair to Lee, that's all.

00:25:25.314 --> 00:25:26.147
- I guess you want.

00:25:26.147 --> 00:25:29.620
So, I think critics of Rank Choice voting

00:25:29.620 --> 00:25:33.770
often bring up the case
of of Burlington, Vermont,

00:25:33.770 --> 00:25:36.620
which was one election
which Ranked Choice Voting

00:25:36.620 --> 00:25:41.620
did yield a weird result,
and no system is perfect.

00:25:43.070 --> 00:25:46.150
I mean, I think we have to
be honest about that upfront,

00:25:46.150 --> 00:25:49.780
that every system, under
certain conditions,

00:25:49.780 --> 00:25:52.650
can yield a an unexpected result.

00:25:52.650 --> 00:25:54.850
If there were a perfect voting system,

00:25:54.850 --> 00:25:57.683
we'd all be using that voting system.

00:25:58.613 --> 00:26:01.740
But you know that's a fundamental fallacy

00:26:01.740 --> 00:26:03.790
of selecting on your dependent variable,

00:26:03.790 --> 00:26:05.450
which is picking the one outcome

00:26:05.450 --> 00:26:08.430
that supports your argument,

00:26:08.430 --> 00:26:13.430
and cherry picking and
ignoring the many number

00:26:13.490 --> 00:26:15.310
of elections that have been held

00:26:15.310 --> 00:26:18.630
under Rank Choice Voting
that have worked just fine.

00:26:18.630 --> 00:26:20.960
And again, Australia has
happily used the system

00:26:20.960 --> 00:26:23.790
for a hundred years, Ireland
has heavily used the system

00:26:23.790 --> 00:26:24.623
for a hundred years.

00:26:24.623 --> 00:26:26.880
It's been used in many
elections in the U.S,

00:26:26.880 --> 00:26:31.880
and in St. Paul, Minneapolis,
Oakland, San Francisco,

00:26:32.250 --> 00:26:34.810
is now being used in
New York in the primary,

00:26:34.810 --> 00:26:36.420
it's in Maine, it's in Alaska.

00:26:36.420 --> 00:26:38.380
And yes, it's not gonna win
every ballot initiative,

00:26:38.380 --> 00:26:40.620
because a lot of people
haven't heard of it,

00:26:40.620 --> 00:26:42.930
and they don't understand it yet,

00:26:42.930 --> 00:26:46.977
but the more people understand it,

00:26:46.977 --> 00:26:49.820
the more people are
enthusiastic about that.

00:26:49.820 --> 00:26:52.750
And I know that having
watched the progress

00:26:52.750 --> 00:26:55.010
of Rank Choice Voting
over the last few years,

00:26:55.010 --> 00:26:57.133
that once people get it, they like it.

00:26:59.250 --> 00:27:00.083
- Okay.

00:27:02.032 --> 00:27:04.232
Are we ready to move
into the next question?

00:27:05.650 --> 00:27:08.310
Aaron, do you wanna
answer the next question?

00:27:08.310 --> 00:27:10.143
Should I restate it?

00:27:11.520 --> 00:27:12.800
- [Aaron] Sure, maybe for the audience.

00:27:12.800 --> 00:27:14.380
- Which method is more resistant

00:27:14.380 --> 00:27:16.533
to strategic voting and why?

00:27:18.730 --> 00:27:21.940
- So, here, when we're talking
about strategic voting,

00:27:21.940 --> 00:27:25.160
the idea of someone voting in a way

00:27:25.160 --> 00:27:28.360
that benefits them overall,

00:27:28.360 --> 00:27:31.740
I think perhaps more important here is

00:27:33.010 --> 00:27:35.810
how does someone
optimizing their own vote,

00:27:35.810 --> 00:27:39.530
voting the way that
they feel is necessary,

00:27:39.530 --> 00:27:42.360
how does that play into the outcome?

00:27:42.360 --> 00:27:44.370
Because if it doesn't give you,

00:27:44.370 --> 00:27:48.530
if the voting method is
robust to strategic voting

00:27:48.530 --> 00:27:51.040
and still gives you a strong outcome,

00:27:51.040 --> 00:27:53.423
then I don't think
there's a big worry there.

00:27:54.260 --> 00:27:57.963
So, with Approval Voting,
I think a lot of the times,

00:27:58.980 --> 00:28:01.093
opponents look at this and say like, well,

00:28:02.270 --> 00:28:04.550
people are just kind of
vote for one candidate

00:28:04.550 --> 00:28:06.520
and that's gonna be it.

00:28:06.520 --> 00:28:09.260
Well, I don't think that's a big deal.

00:28:09.260 --> 00:28:13.720
In some cases, it's gonna make
sense for an individual voter

00:28:13.720 --> 00:28:15.740
to vote for one candidate.

00:28:15.740 --> 00:28:18.290
If that makes sense for them,
that makes sense for them.

00:28:18.290 --> 00:28:21.950
You can still have, even if
say, three quarters of people

00:28:21.950 --> 00:28:23.230
are choosing only one candidate,

00:28:23.230 --> 00:28:27.963
and then some elections that
may make sense for most voters.

00:28:29.310 --> 00:28:31.383
But even with that remainder,

00:28:32.300 --> 00:28:35.480
that's enough to cover
the margin of victory

00:28:35.480 --> 00:28:38.781
to change the winner, if
indeed a different winner

00:28:38.781 --> 00:28:41.430
should be necessary.

00:28:41.430 --> 00:28:46.160
It also makes it so that
the other candidates

00:28:46.160 --> 00:28:49.150
are able to get their
reflection support heard.

00:28:49.150 --> 00:28:52.630
So, overall, I'm not concerned with this.

00:28:52.630 --> 00:28:54.791
As long as you are getting strong winners,

00:28:54.791 --> 00:28:56.190
that's the main concern.

00:28:56.190 --> 00:28:58.950
And as long as other candidates

00:28:58.950 --> 00:29:02.230
are getting the accurate
reflection of support,

00:29:02.230 --> 00:29:04.730
then I think the voting
method is doing its job.

00:29:04.730 --> 00:29:07.720
I would be more concerned
again with the voting method

00:29:07.720 --> 00:29:10.260
that when people are voting honestly,

00:29:10.260 --> 00:29:12.540
that they get a worse outcome.

00:29:12.540 --> 00:29:14.130
And again, like thinking in Burlington.

00:29:14.130 --> 00:29:15.810
And we have to pick on
things like Burlington,

00:29:15.810 --> 00:29:18.620
because again, nine out of ten times,

00:29:18.620 --> 00:29:20.530
these voting methods are
gonna get the same result.

00:29:20.530 --> 00:29:24.570
So, we have to kind of focus on that 10%,

00:29:24.570 --> 00:29:28.040
and as Rank Choice Voting is
implemented in more places,

00:29:28.040 --> 00:29:30.370
we're gonna see more Burlingtons,

00:29:30.370 --> 00:29:32.980
but really we wanna
focus on a voting method

00:29:32.980 --> 00:29:35.790
that gets a good outcome and does its job

00:29:35.790 --> 00:29:38.300
on being able to capture
candidate support.

00:29:38.300 --> 00:29:40.990
And those are areas where Approval Voting,

00:29:40.990 --> 00:29:42.263
hands down, does better.

00:29:47.560 --> 00:29:50.020
- I just wanna flag something here,

00:29:50.020 --> 00:29:52.620
because I'm a little confused.

00:29:52.620 --> 00:29:54.720
I'm hearing terms like "good outcome"

00:29:54.720 --> 00:29:57.090
and I haven't heard those defined yet.

00:29:57.090 --> 00:29:59.380
I'm hearing terms like "strong candidate",

00:29:59.380 --> 00:30:02.620
and I haven't heard those defined yet.

00:30:02.620 --> 00:30:07.620
So, I hope Aaron will define
those in his rebuttal here.

00:30:10.989 --> 00:30:14.413
I mean, Aaron is telling us
that nine times out of ten,

00:30:15.372 --> 00:30:16.980
Rank Choice Voting and Approval Voting

00:30:16.980 --> 00:30:19.620
will get the same result.

00:30:19.620 --> 00:30:22.920
And I don't know what that's based on,

00:30:22.920 --> 00:30:27.920
because they haven't been
run in the same elections.

00:30:27.940 --> 00:30:30.220
It might be based on hypothetical polling,

00:30:30.220 --> 00:30:34.143
or modeling, but that's
not the real world.

00:30:35.693 --> 00:30:40.043
The argument that I'm
hearing right now is that,

00:30:41.400 --> 00:30:43.420
we're really focusing on the voter,

00:30:43.420 --> 00:30:46.750
and the voters satisfaction,
and the voters are important,

00:30:46.750 --> 00:30:49.920
but they're also part of a
broader political system.

00:30:49.920 --> 00:30:52.500
And right now we have a
very broken and destructive

00:30:52.500 --> 00:30:56.470
political system that is highly
polarized, highly binary,

00:30:56.470 --> 00:30:57.950
highly divisive.

00:30:57.950 --> 00:31:01.720
And so, I think we ought to think about

00:31:01.720 --> 00:31:04.890
which political system is gonna
help solve those problems.

00:31:04.890 --> 00:31:09.240
But this question is on the
sincere versus strategic voting.

00:31:09.240 --> 00:31:13.150
And I'm actually not sure that, I mean,

00:31:13.150 --> 00:31:16.530
this is all hypothetical,
because we don't know,

00:31:16.530 --> 00:31:19.400
because we just don't
have a lot of experience

00:31:19.400 --> 00:31:24.400
with Approval Voting, that
the results would be the same.

00:31:24.600 --> 00:31:28.750
But I just wanna, because we
need hypothetical examples,

00:31:28.750 --> 00:31:30.090
I just wanna take what I think might be

00:31:30.090 --> 00:31:33.703
a realistic hypothetical
example, which is to say that,

00:31:34.780 --> 00:31:38.490
Aaron, Lisa, and I are
all running for Senator,

00:31:38.490 --> 00:31:41.050
and I'm on the left, and
Aaron's on the right,

00:31:41.050 --> 00:31:43.690
and Lisa's in the middle, and
she's gotta be the moderate,

00:31:43.690 --> 00:31:47.373
'cause she's the moderator,
don't all laugh at once.

00:31:49.310 --> 00:31:52.160
And let's say this audience is
like the American electorate,

00:31:52.160 --> 00:31:55.860
40% left, 20% center, 40% right,

00:31:55.860 --> 00:31:58.890
and yeah, probably if everybody
were voting sincerely,

00:31:58.890 --> 00:32:00.370
Lisa should be the winner.

00:32:00.370 --> 00:32:05.370
But here's the problem,
is that I would know that.

00:32:05.560 --> 00:32:09.250
So, I got to tell my
supporters don't vote for Lisa,

00:32:09.250 --> 00:32:11.230
because I think I can
beat Aaron Head to head

00:32:11.230 --> 00:32:14.810
if you all turn out and
nobody votes for Lisa,

00:32:14.810 --> 00:32:18.220
because she would get her 20% plus my 40%,

00:32:18.220 --> 00:32:21.410
then she's gonna beat me, and
Aaron strategy's the same.

00:32:21.410 --> 00:32:22.710
So, he's gonna tell his supporters

00:32:22.710 --> 00:32:24.630
not to vote for Lisa too,
because he knows this,

00:32:24.630 --> 00:32:26.253
and then I know this.

00:32:26.253 --> 00:32:27.830
So, I know that definitely
his supporters...

00:32:27.830 --> 00:32:30.930
So, you can game this out.

00:32:30.930 --> 00:32:34.550
And what we wind up is,
that we've devolved back

00:32:34.550 --> 00:32:38.260
into standard plurality, zero sum voting.

00:32:38.260 --> 00:32:41.620
And this is the experience
of Approval Voting,

00:32:41.620 --> 00:32:45.450
is that often just devolves back into

00:32:45.450 --> 00:32:48.430
a Bullet voting with
the same demonization.

00:32:48.430 --> 00:32:50.630
And frankly, I think Lisa's pretty smart,

00:32:50.630 --> 00:32:53.480
and she might realize what
was gonna happen to her,

00:32:53.480 --> 00:32:56.253
and she's not gonna even
run under this scenario.

00:32:57.100 --> 00:32:59.814
Now, RCV is gonna be different because

00:32:59.814 --> 00:33:03.480
it's possible that Lisa will still get

00:33:03.480 --> 00:33:06.340
the 20% first preference choices.

00:33:06.340 --> 00:33:08.290
But now, she's a lot more important,

00:33:08.290 --> 00:33:11.460
because Aaron and I both
are gonna really want

00:33:11.460 --> 00:33:13.330
her second choices, and
we have no incentive

00:33:13.330 --> 00:33:16.140
to demonize her, 'cause
we already have our 40%.

00:33:16.140 --> 00:33:19.280
What we want is to move towards the middle

00:33:19.280 --> 00:33:22.160
and get her supporters,
and build coalitions.

00:33:22.160 --> 00:33:27.160
And this is why RCV is
superior in my mind,

00:33:27.490 --> 00:33:31.470
is because it encourages
even candidates on the edges

00:33:31.470 --> 00:33:34.760
to work towards building
a broader coalition.

00:33:34.760 --> 00:33:37.140
In Approval Voting, the goal of candidates

00:33:37.140 --> 00:33:38.990
is to tell all their supporters,

00:33:38.990 --> 00:33:39.890
or dominant candidates,

00:33:39.890 --> 00:33:42.200
tell their supporters to bullet vote.

00:33:42.200 --> 00:33:46.270
In Rank Choice voting, I'm trying to build

00:33:46.270 --> 00:33:47.460
a broader coalition.

00:33:47.460 --> 00:33:49.950
So, I think when we think
about these systems,

00:33:49.950 --> 00:33:51.730
we have to think of voting systems,

00:33:51.730 --> 00:33:54.150
we have to think about
them as a broader part

00:33:54.150 --> 00:33:55.300
of the political systems.

00:33:55.300 --> 00:33:58.140
We have to think about how
campaigns are gonna operate,

00:33:58.140 --> 00:34:00.410
we have to think how
candidates are gonna operate,

00:34:00.410 --> 00:34:01.650
and we have to think how voters

00:34:01.650 --> 00:34:03.410
are gonna be responsive to that.

00:34:03.410 --> 00:34:07.210
And the dominant strategy
in Approval Voting

00:34:07.210 --> 00:34:08.920
is just to bullet vote.

00:34:08.920 --> 00:34:10.380
And if Aaron's fine with that,

00:34:10.380 --> 00:34:13.440
then I don't know why we
even bothered to change

00:34:13.440 --> 00:34:15.480
to Approval Voting, because then,

00:34:15.480 --> 00:34:17.890
we might as well just
stick with Plurality voting

00:34:17.890 --> 00:34:19.653
and save us the hassle.

00:34:21.300 --> 00:34:22.133
- Aaron.

00:34:26.490 --> 00:34:27.323
- Yeah.

00:34:27.323 --> 00:34:29.620
So Lee asked, well, what
makes a good winner?

00:34:29.620 --> 00:34:32.010
I think that's a fair question.

00:34:32.010 --> 00:34:36.990
So, a good winter is one
that has the maximum support

00:34:36.990 --> 00:34:39.600
of the electorate over
any other candidate.

00:34:39.600 --> 00:34:43.680
And when we do polling,
we ask people as well,

00:34:43.680 --> 00:34:45.320
we asked their honest opinion,

00:34:45.320 --> 00:34:47.300
along with how they would vote

00:34:47.300 --> 00:34:48.997
under different elections scenarios.

00:34:48.997 --> 00:34:52.060
And Approval Voting, much
more than Rank Choice Voting,

00:34:52.060 --> 00:34:53.880
it lines up with people's honest opinion.

00:34:53.880 --> 00:34:56.010
We ask people that in St. Louis as well,

00:34:56.010 --> 00:34:59.400
their Approval Voting support lined up

00:34:59.400 --> 00:35:01.200
with their honest assessment.

00:35:01.200 --> 00:35:03.580
So, and that was even...

00:35:03.580 --> 00:35:07.665
And also people voted for 1.6 candidates

00:35:07.665 --> 00:35:10.290
per ballot in St. Louis.

00:35:10.290 --> 00:35:13.610
So even there, like we're
hardly seeing Bullet voting

00:35:13.610 --> 00:35:15.640
being rampant, but if it was like,

00:35:15.640 --> 00:35:17.610
maybe it makes sense for
those voters as well.

00:35:17.610 --> 00:35:19.057
What's important is that
we get good winners,

00:35:19.057 --> 00:35:21.000
and we can measure good winners

00:35:21.000 --> 00:35:22.773
while we're doing this polling.

00:35:24.650 --> 00:35:26.510
And so, that's what I would say.

00:35:26.510 --> 00:35:29.830
So, if you're getting
good results, really,

00:35:29.830 --> 00:35:31.503
that's really all that matters.

00:35:33.110 --> 00:35:33.943
- Okay.

00:35:34.810 --> 00:35:37.280
Are we ready to move on
to the next question?

00:35:40.220 --> 00:35:43.500
And we're a little squeezed on our time.

00:35:43.500 --> 00:35:46.150
So, I'm going to ask a question,

00:35:46.150 --> 00:35:49.130
and ask that each of you watch your time

00:35:49.130 --> 00:35:52.230
on answer and rebuttal.

00:35:52.230 --> 00:35:53.140
The next question:

00:35:53.140 --> 00:35:56.420
Which method produces
more moderate winners,

00:35:56.420 --> 00:35:58.183
and is this always good?

00:35:59.110 --> 00:36:00.460
Lee, do you wanna kick off?

00:36:01.550 --> 00:36:02.630
- Sure.

00:36:02.630 --> 00:36:03.744
Yeah.

00:36:03.744 --> 00:36:06.560
And again, I keep coming back to this,

00:36:06.560 --> 00:36:09.190
but I think this is an
incredibly important point,

00:36:09.190 --> 00:36:11.320
that with Rank Choice Voting,

00:36:11.320 --> 00:36:14.900
we don't have to rely
on hypothetical polls

00:36:14.900 --> 00:36:17.060
that are divorced from
the rough and tumble

00:36:17.060 --> 00:36:18.830
of actual campaigns.

00:36:18.830 --> 00:36:23.830
We can actually look at the
record and we can look at

00:36:23.920 --> 00:36:26.210
a hundred years of moderate
Australian politics,

00:36:26.210 --> 00:36:27.290
we can look at a hundred years

00:36:27.290 --> 00:36:29.768
of relatively moderate Irish politics,

00:36:29.768 --> 00:36:33.399
and we we don't know,
from Approval Voting,

00:36:33.399 --> 00:36:36.250
because there's just no history there.

00:36:36.250 --> 00:36:38.030
We can do polls all we want,

00:36:38.030 --> 00:36:40.705
but polls are actually...

00:36:40.705 --> 00:36:42.598
Aaron was talking about a good way of...

00:36:42.598 --> 00:36:45.460
we wanna know what candidate
support actually is.

00:36:45.460 --> 00:36:48.700
Well, actually pulling is
a good way to know that.

00:36:48.700 --> 00:36:51.780
We don't, so, we have a way to do that.

00:36:51.780 --> 00:36:56.510
I just wanna talk about
the St. Louis election,

00:36:56.510 --> 00:36:59.550
'cause that is the case
that we have most recently

00:36:59.550 --> 00:37:01.517
of Approval Voting.

00:37:01.517 --> 00:37:05.140
And I wanna know that that
was a two candidate election,

00:37:05.140 --> 00:37:07.020
not a Single Winner candidate election.

00:37:07.020 --> 00:37:09.060
So, actually one point, even though voters

00:37:09.060 --> 00:37:10.610
were selecting two candidates,

00:37:10.610 --> 00:37:12.933
they actually under voted at 1.6.

00:37:13.990 --> 00:37:17.060
And actually, turn out
went down in St. Louis.

00:37:17.060 --> 00:37:20.960
So, there was this 22%
turnout in this election.

00:37:20.960 --> 00:37:25.053
It was actually down 11,000
votes from four years ago.

00:37:26.468 --> 00:37:30.080
And so, I think with RCV,

00:37:30.080 --> 00:37:35.080
you actually get more
people using those rankings.

00:37:36.530 --> 00:37:39.250
And so, you're more likely to figure out

00:37:39.250 --> 00:37:41.710
what the moderate winner is.

00:37:41.710 --> 00:37:42.888
And I think again,

00:37:42.888 --> 00:37:45.830
I'm finding it hard to understand

00:37:45.830 --> 00:37:49.210
what Aaron really talks
about a good outcome

00:37:49.210 --> 00:37:52.450
is who who has maximal
support among the electorate.

00:37:52.450 --> 00:37:55.170
I think what he's trying to get at is

00:37:55.170 --> 00:37:57.600
who would be the condersay winner,

00:37:57.600 --> 00:37:59.410
which is the idea who would who would win

00:37:59.410 --> 00:38:02.258
the most head to head match-ups,

00:38:02.258 --> 00:38:05.730
which is a sort of gold standard

00:38:05.730 --> 00:38:08.350
for how you measure electoral systems,

00:38:08.350 --> 00:38:10.790
but it's also impossible to achieve,

00:38:10.790 --> 00:38:12.990
unless everybody ranks every candidate,

00:38:12.990 --> 00:38:17.310
which is just not gonna happen.

00:38:17.310 --> 00:38:22.310
So, I'm just perplexed there I think.

00:38:23.943 --> 00:38:26.870
I mean, there is a fundamental
problem, of course,

00:38:26.870 --> 00:38:30.220
in getting moderate
winners, which is that,

00:38:30.220 --> 00:38:33.790
there's tremendous
polarization in our society.

00:38:33.790 --> 00:38:36.460
And if the electorate is divided,

00:38:36.460 --> 00:38:41.460
it's gonna be really hard
to find a moderate winner.

00:38:41.950 --> 00:38:43.840
And frankly, this is
the fundamental problem

00:38:43.840 --> 00:38:46.240
with Single Winner election systems,

00:38:46.240 --> 00:38:48.800
and I hope we'll debate, at a later point,

00:38:48.800 --> 00:38:51.800
the value of multi winner
proportional electoral systems

00:38:51.800 --> 00:38:53.310
which are really important

00:38:53.310 --> 00:38:56.200
for generating more moderate winners.

00:38:56.200 --> 00:38:57.990
But I think we have to
work from the reality

00:38:57.990 --> 00:38:59.310
of where we are.

00:38:59.310 --> 00:39:02.280
And so, the question is not
necessarily which is the system

00:39:02.280 --> 00:39:04.740
that's gonna most elect the most moderate.

00:39:04.740 --> 00:39:06.640
So, though I think Rank
Choice voting would do that,

00:39:06.640 --> 00:39:08.480
but more importantly, wishes the system

00:39:08.480 --> 00:39:11.440
that's going to incentivize
the most moderation,

00:39:11.440 --> 00:39:14.840
given that we have an electorate
that is deeply polarized.

00:39:14.840 --> 00:39:18.450
And again, for reasons
that I hope are clear,

00:39:18.450 --> 00:39:20.260
I think that system is Rank Choice Voting,

00:39:20.260 --> 00:39:23.176
because it encourages candidates to build

00:39:23.176 --> 00:39:25.610
the broadest coalition to ensure that

00:39:25.610 --> 00:39:27.950
they have majority support.

00:39:27.950 --> 00:39:30.170
Whereas in Approval Voting,

00:39:30.170 --> 00:39:34.440
there is a strategic decision
of voters in campaigns

00:39:34.440 --> 00:39:37.470
to try to get their
supporters to bullet vote,

00:39:37.470 --> 00:39:39.410
which again creates the same divisive

00:39:39.410 --> 00:39:43.943
zero sum dissatisfaction
problems of Plurality voting.

00:39:46.310 --> 00:39:47.143
- Thanks.

00:39:47.143 --> 00:39:48.730
Aaron.

00:39:48.730 --> 00:39:49.760
- Sure.

00:39:49.760 --> 00:39:53.850
So, when I said that a good winner

00:39:53.850 --> 00:39:56.780
is one that maximizes the
support from the electorate,

00:39:56.780 --> 00:39:57.730
that's what I meant.

00:39:57.730 --> 00:39:59.740
Condersay winter is
another way of doing it,

00:39:59.740 --> 00:40:03.160
but we need to talk
about voter satisfaction.

00:40:03.160 --> 00:40:05.923
And so, when I said that,
that's what I meant.

00:40:07.588 --> 00:40:10.560
And when we're talking about
getting moderate winners,

00:40:10.560 --> 00:40:12.473
really like we're talking about getting

00:40:12.473 --> 00:40:16.560
the candidate who has the most support

00:40:19.280 --> 00:40:20.730
from the electorate themselves.

00:40:20.730 --> 00:40:23.640
And moderate is going to
vary from place to place.

00:40:23.640 --> 00:40:26.170
So, really, when it comes down
to it, that's what we want.

00:40:26.170 --> 00:40:27.740
We want a strong consensus winner

00:40:27.740 --> 00:40:30.443
when we're talking about
who we want to win.

00:40:32.230 --> 00:40:36.007
So, Lee mentioned that St.
Louis was a two-person election.

00:40:36.007 --> 00:40:39.340
So, here we have Approval
Voting being used,

00:40:39.340 --> 00:40:40.910
we can choose as many
candidates as you want,

00:40:40.910 --> 00:40:43.830
and then it goes to a runoff in St. Louis.

00:40:43.830 --> 00:40:47.580
But I thought there might be
some naysayers ahead of time.

00:40:47.580 --> 00:40:50.710
So, unfortunately, when we
were doing our research design

00:40:50.710 --> 00:40:53.930
in St. Louis, we also asked
them how they would vote

00:40:53.930 --> 00:40:57.510
if there wasn't a runoff, would
they have voted differently?

00:40:57.510 --> 00:41:00.118
And indeed some people would
have voted differently.

00:41:00.118 --> 00:41:04.250
The votes per ballot would
have dropped slightly from 1.6

00:41:04.250 --> 00:41:09.030
to about 1.5, but ultimately
we have the same winner,

00:41:09.030 --> 00:41:11.890
same winner order, and
roughly the same support

00:41:11.890 --> 00:41:14.310
among all the candidates as well.

00:41:14.310 --> 00:41:17.690
And it's important to do these polls

00:41:17.690 --> 00:41:20.950
alongside real elections,
because otherwise,

00:41:20.950 --> 00:41:25.095
you run into a fantastical hypotheticals

00:41:25.095 --> 00:41:26.700
that folks dream up.

00:41:26.700 --> 00:41:28.923
And so when we look at
a particular election,

00:41:28.923 --> 00:41:30.960
what we do is we ask people, okay, well,

00:41:30.960 --> 00:41:33.290
here's an option using a
particular voting method.

00:41:33.290 --> 00:41:36.280
How would you have voted if
this was the voting method?

00:41:36.280 --> 00:41:39.700
And then we also asked them
what their honest assessment is

00:41:39.700 --> 00:41:40.533
of these candidates.

00:41:40.533 --> 00:41:42.150
And we ask them that in different ways.

00:41:42.150 --> 00:41:46.020
We ask them by using a Likert type scale,

00:41:46.020 --> 00:41:49.440
we asked them by ranking
their honest rankings,

00:41:49.440 --> 00:41:51.788
and they ask them who
they honestly approve of.

00:41:51.788 --> 00:41:53.910
And when we looked at that compared

00:41:53.910 --> 00:41:56.682
to the Approval Voting
data through the election,

00:41:56.682 --> 00:41:58.130
it worked out well.

00:41:58.130 --> 00:42:00.303
Like those lined up.

00:42:03.033 --> 00:42:05.090
And there are a number of
reasons why Rank Scale Voting

00:42:05.090 --> 00:42:06.870
may struggle here.

00:42:06.870 --> 00:42:10.540
One is that, it's not very
good data to begin with.

00:42:10.540 --> 00:42:11.820
When you're looking at rankings,

00:42:11.820 --> 00:42:13.540
you don't know where the threshold

00:42:13.540 --> 00:42:15.250
is for where someone approves a candidate

00:42:15.250 --> 00:42:16.930
and and where they don't.

00:42:16.930 --> 00:42:19.260
So, you're fundamentally
not having information

00:42:19.260 --> 00:42:21.650
to begin, with, and also the
method is so complicated,

00:42:21.650 --> 00:42:24.700
that many cities truncate
the amount of information

00:42:24.700 --> 00:42:25.750
that you can provide.

00:42:25.750 --> 00:42:28.940
So, if you don't get the
information to begin with,

00:42:28.940 --> 00:42:32.290
of course, you're gonna have
trouble getting a good result.

00:42:32.290 --> 00:42:35.010
So, even when a voting method
like Rank Choice Voting

00:42:35.010 --> 00:42:37.060
appears to be getting good results,

00:42:37.060 --> 00:42:38.320
well like we don't always know.

00:42:38.320 --> 00:42:40.690
Because like, there's
a lack of information.

00:42:40.690 --> 00:42:44.102
And when you have more
candidates, which by the way,

00:42:44.102 --> 00:42:47.450
cities on average, have
eight mayoral candidates,

00:42:47.450 --> 00:42:50.001
good luck doing that with a
Rank Choice Voting election

00:42:50.001 --> 00:42:54.380
when people get tired of breaking
through those candidates.

00:42:54.380 --> 00:42:56.420
It's much easier to do
that with Approval Voting

00:42:56.420 --> 00:42:58.230
even when you have a long candidate list.

00:42:58.230 --> 00:43:00.430
And that's how you get
these better results.

00:43:02.180 --> 00:43:03.440
- Okay.

00:43:03.440 --> 00:43:06.450
Lee, did you have a
rebuttal or are we ready?

00:43:06.450 --> 00:43:11.450
- I mean, I think we're arguing
angels on pin with this.

00:43:13.670 --> 00:43:16.700
What counts as a better result.

00:43:16.700 --> 00:43:19.549
I mean, Approval Voting assumes,

00:43:19.549 --> 00:43:21.990
I mean, when we're talking
about voter preference

00:43:21.990 --> 00:43:26.100
and measuring that, I think
Approval Voting assumes that

00:43:26.100 --> 00:43:28.500
you either like a candidate
or you don't like a candidate.

00:43:28.500 --> 00:43:31.670
But Rank Choice Voting data is,

00:43:31.670 --> 00:43:33.750
I think actually quite more informative,

00:43:33.750 --> 00:43:36.310
because you are much more likely,

00:43:36.310 --> 00:43:39.950
people mostly use their ranking.

00:43:39.950 --> 00:43:42.040
So, people are much more likely to vote

00:43:42.040 --> 00:43:44.680
for multiple candidates
under ranked systems,

00:43:44.680 --> 00:43:48.107
and you actually have an
incredible trove of data

00:43:51.030 --> 00:43:53.190
to see who were first preferences,

00:43:53.190 --> 00:43:55.800
who were second preferences,
who were third preferences,

00:43:55.800 --> 00:44:00.800
and you can vote for more
candidates down the ballot

00:44:00.978 --> 00:44:03.540
without harming your favorite,

00:44:03.540 --> 00:44:06.050
if your favorite is a leading candidate.

00:44:06.050 --> 00:44:08.090
And that actually, I think,

00:44:08.090 --> 00:44:12.860
generates a better sense
of who voters prefer

00:44:16.010 --> 00:44:18.160
and broad support.

00:44:18.160 --> 00:44:22.340
But, again, I think the fundamental thing

00:44:22.340 --> 00:44:24.930
that's happening in this debate,

00:44:24.930 --> 00:44:27.740
that I'm finding a little frustrating is

00:44:29.040 --> 00:44:32.040
it seems like Aaron is trying
to abstract voting system

00:44:32.040 --> 00:44:35.310
outside of the politics,
and kind of talk about

00:44:35.310 --> 00:44:37.140
a pure condersay winner.

00:44:37.140 --> 00:44:42.140
And I think it is important
to think about the elections

00:44:45.620 --> 00:44:47.600
as part of a political process.

00:44:47.600 --> 00:44:49.860
So, it's part of how people campaign,

00:44:49.860 --> 00:44:52.360
it's part of how people govern,

00:44:52.360 --> 00:44:56.203
and it's part of how voters engage.

00:44:58.004 --> 00:45:00.240
And Rank Choice Voting is a way for voters

00:45:00.240 --> 00:45:03.410
to be very expressive, and a
way for candidates to reach out

00:45:03.410 --> 00:45:04.670
to a lot of different candidates.

00:45:04.670 --> 00:45:07.070
And you survey voters in cities

00:45:07.070 --> 00:45:11.020
that have used Rank Choice
Voting, and 90, 91, 92%

00:45:11.020 --> 00:45:12.940
of people say they were satisfied.

00:45:12.940 --> 00:45:14.570
They say it was easy.

00:45:14.570 --> 00:45:18.360
It's a myth that people find it hard.

00:45:18.360 --> 00:45:19.550
There's few people who find it hard

00:45:19.550 --> 00:45:20.550
the first time they do it,

00:45:20.550 --> 00:45:22.895
but it's really not that hard to rank.

00:45:22.895 --> 00:45:24.760
We rank all the time.

00:45:24.760 --> 00:45:26.000
What are your 10 favorite movies?

00:45:26.000 --> 00:45:27.630
What are your 10 favorite books?

00:45:27.630 --> 00:45:29.680
What are your 10 favorite beers?

00:45:29.680 --> 00:45:33.063
So, I'm just a little perplexed here.

00:45:34.100 --> 00:45:34.933
- All right.

00:45:34.933 --> 00:45:35.890
So, I'll tell you what we're gonna do.

00:45:35.890 --> 00:45:38.803
We're gonna table the
discussion for the moment

00:45:38.803 --> 00:45:41.670
on which system is better,

00:45:41.670 --> 00:45:45.010
and let's go to the next topic.

00:45:45.010 --> 00:45:49.970
Suppose you convinced the
audience that your method is best,

00:45:49.970 --> 00:45:52.140
what are the biggest obstacles

00:45:52.140 --> 00:45:55.660
to reforming the existing system,

00:45:55.660 --> 00:45:58.020
how can they be overcome,

00:45:58.020 --> 00:46:00.623
and what can our audience do to help?

00:46:01.840 --> 00:46:03.060
Lee, do you wanna start?

00:46:03.060 --> 00:46:05.840
And you'll get three minutes to respond.

00:46:05.840 --> 00:46:06.673
- Yeah.

00:46:06.673 --> 00:46:10.530
Well, yeah, I think we're
at a real inflection point

00:46:10.530 --> 00:46:12.770
in our democracy in that,

00:46:12.770 --> 00:46:16.470
we're having a very broad
democracy reform conversation.

00:46:16.470 --> 00:46:20.720
And some of that is around
the immediate challenges

00:46:20.720 --> 00:46:24.040
of voter suppression,

00:46:24.040 --> 00:46:29.040
and Georgia just passed a
very restrictive voter ID law,

00:46:29.422 --> 00:46:33.740
and the Democrats response
for the people at H.R.1.

00:46:33.740 --> 00:46:36.450
And we're really thinking
about what it means

00:46:36.450 --> 00:46:38.107
to have a fair democracy.

00:46:38.107 --> 00:46:43.107
And I think this is a decade
long conversation, at least.

00:46:44.327 --> 00:46:49.260
And so, I think it's
important to think about this

00:46:49.260 --> 00:46:52.200
in a broader context, that this is not

00:46:52.200 --> 00:46:55.770
just about Rank Choice
Voting or Approval Voting,

00:46:55.770 --> 00:46:59.840
it's about what is gonna make
for a fair, representative,

00:46:59.840 --> 00:47:02.960
just, equitable democracy,
particularly in this moment

00:47:02.960 --> 00:47:06.570
in which we are transitioning as a society

00:47:06.570 --> 00:47:08.600
to being a multi-ethnic democracy,

00:47:08.600 --> 00:47:10.839
and really dealing with some of these

00:47:10.839 --> 00:47:15.839
very nasty and ugly legacies
of earlier regressive

00:47:18.130 --> 00:47:20.263
Jim Crow eras in our politics.

00:47:21.280 --> 00:47:26.280
So, I wanna make sure that we
are having these conversations

00:47:26.797 --> 00:47:29.780
as part of that bigger conversation,

00:47:29.780 --> 00:47:31.280
and I think it's important in this moment

00:47:31.280 --> 00:47:34.770
to have a broad conversation

00:47:34.770 --> 00:47:39.480
about alternative voting
systems, which we are doing now.

00:47:39.480 --> 00:47:41.480
And I think one of the challenges is that,

00:47:41.480 --> 00:47:43.490
we have to understand
some of the ways in which

00:47:43.490 --> 00:47:46.240
our voting system itself is contributing

00:47:46.240 --> 00:47:47.670
to our political problems.

00:47:47.670 --> 00:47:49.400
As you mentioned, I'm the author,

00:47:49.400 --> 00:47:51.927
of the book "Breaking
the Two-Party Doom Loop:

00:47:51.927 --> 00:47:55.150
"The Case for Multiparty
Democracy in America"

00:47:55.150 --> 00:47:56.940
in which I argue that,

00:47:56.940 --> 00:47:58.670
it is in fact, our two party system

00:47:58.670 --> 00:48:02.420
that is fostering a
incredible divisiveness,

00:48:02.420 --> 00:48:05.780
and that a multi-party system
I think would work much better

00:48:05.780 --> 00:48:08.239
because it breaks the binary nature

00:48:08.239 --> 00:48:11.660
of our political conflict,
which is exacerbating

00:48:11.660 --> 00:48:16.360
so many of these us versus them problems,

00:48:16.360 --> 00:48:20.280
and also is allowing a political minority

00:48:20.280 --> 00:48:21.530
to have outsized power,

00:48:21.530 --> 00:48:24.350
because it's well-placed in our system.

00:48:24.350 --> 00:48:26.720
And here, I think we also
ought to be thinking about

00:48:26.720 --> 00:48:28.330
proportional representation.

00:48:28.330 --> 00:48:30.090
I mean, I think there are real...

00:48:30.090 --> 00:48:32.880
We're having this debate
about Single Winner systems.

00:48:32.880 --> 00:48:35.440
I think there are real limits
to Single Winner elections,

00:48:35.440 --> 00:48:38.270
although there are certainly
certain electoral offices

00:48:38.270 --> 00:48:40.050
that require Single Winners.

00:48:40.050 --> 00:48:42.860
So, I think we all also
have to think about

00:48:42.860 --> 00:48:46.360
the equity aspects of this, and this case,

00:48:46.360 --> 00:48:49.450
I think particularly,
proportional Rank Choice Voting

00:48:49.450 --> 00:48:52.560
is a very good system
for a diverse society.

00:48:52.560 --> 00:48:57.560
And again, I go back to what
election experts talk about,

00:48:58.080 --> 00:49:02.630
is that, if you have a
divided pluralistic society,

00:49:02.630 --> 00:49:04.636
you want a proportional system,

00:49:04.636 --> 00:49:07.120
and probably a Ranked
Choice Voting system too

00:49:07.120 --> 00:49:11.460
in order to build cross
coalitional support.

00:49:11.460 --> 00:49:14.480
So, I think it's important

00:49:14.480 --> 00:49:16.390
to get engaged in these conversations.

00:49:16.390 --> 00:49:21.040
I think the biggest obstacle
is a lack of imagination,

00:49:21.040 --> 00:49:24.200
a sense that just because
we have the status quo,

00:49:24.200 --> 00:49:27.460
that's just the only
possibility that there is.

00:49:27.460 --> 00:49:31.220
And this is a moment in which I'm thrilled

00:49:31.220 --> 00:49:33.550
that we're having these
debates, because I think

00:49:33.550 --> 00:49:36.660
it's a real moment to
look at what's gone wrong

00:49:36.660 --> 00:49:39.683
with our political system,
which is clearly declining,

00:49:40.930 --> 00:49:43.180
and if you ask me, you
can look at Freedom House

00:49:43.180 --> 00:49:44.950
or any international rating,

00:49:44.950 --> 00:49:47.270
now everybody's looking
at the U.S and saying,

00:49:47.270 --> 00:49:48.560
what's going wrong.

00:49:48.560 --> 00:49:53.140
So, we have to expand our imagination.

00:49:53.140 --> 00:49:56.085
I think that's the biggest obstacle.

00:49:56.085 --> 00:49:56.918
- That's the biggest obstacle.

00:49:56.918 --> 00:49:57.751
Okay.

00:49:57.751 --> 00:49:58.910
Aron, how about you?

00:49:58.910 --> 00:50:00.230
- I guess I would ask first

00:50:00.230 --> 00:50:02.780
so we don't complete a Rank Choice Voting,

00:50:02.780 --> 00:50:05.860
or instant runoff voting, and
single transferrable vote.

00:50:05.860 --> 00:50:07.396
Those are very different systems.

00:50:07.396 --> 00:50:12.396
So, here, we're talking
about Rank Choice Voting,

00:50:12.630 --> 00:50:16.100
Single Winner, versus Approval Voting.

00:50:16.100 --> 00:50:18.450
And when we're looking at this,

00:50:18.450 --> 00:50:21.470
I think there are two obstacles really,

00:50:21.470 --> 00:50:25.730
in terms of like, and these
are in terms of like arguments

00:50:25.730 --> 00:50:27.420
that are push against Approval Voting.

00:50:27.420 --> 00:50:31.450
And here we here issues about
momentum and expression,

00:50:31.450 --> 00:50:33.960
but first like we need
to recognize that we have

00:50:33.960 --> 00:50:36.470
more options than Rank Choice Voting.

00:50:36.470 --> 00:50:38.490
This is not the only option.

00:50:38.490 --> 00:50:41.010
Approval Voting is way easier,

00:50:41.010 --> 00:50:44.023
and it's free to implement.

00:50:45.390 --> 00:50:49.730
So, we need to be able to keep
this in mind as an option.

00:50:49.730 --> 00:50:51.130
In terms of momentum.

00:50:51.130 --> 00:50:55.990
Well, the RCV folks
collectively have gotten over

00:50:55.990 --> 00:51:00.810
a hundred million dollars over
the last couple of decades.

00:51:00.810 --> 00:51:03.420
You give any kind of intervention,

00:51:03.420 --> 00:51:06.370
that kind of funding and timeline,

00:51:06.370 --> 00:51:10.500
I'd hope that they'd make
some kind of progress.

00:51:10.500 --> 00:51:14.990
But the question is, given
all that funding and timeline,

00:51:14.990 --> 00:51:17.340
why aren't they further than they are?

00:51:17.340 --> 00:51:19.917
You look at Approval
Voting, as an organization,

00:51:19.917 --> 00:51:24.300
we've been around with
funding for three years.

00:51:24.300 --> 00:51:25.556
And within those three years,

00:51:25.556 --> 00:51:30.450
we've helped over 400,000
people get Approval Voting

00:51:30.450 --> 00:51:34.860
to help give them a
voice in their democracy.

00:51:34.860 --> 00:51:39.860
So, sure like RCB is
further along, but like,

00:51:40.130 --> 00:51:42.630
look at the headstart that I got.

00:51:42.630 --> 00:51:44.810
So, I wouldn't really call that momentum,

00:51:44.810 --> 00:51:45.830
particularly when you're looking

00:51:45.830 --> 00:51:47.920
at recent failures like Massachusetts,

00:51:47.920 --> 00:51:50.463
where it lost by 10 points.

00:51:52.099 --> 00:51:54.260
That doesn't look like momentum to me.

00:51:54.260 --> 00:51:56.750
And as far as expressivity

00:51:57.867 --> 00:52:00.700
which Lee had brought up earlier,

00:52:00.700 --> 00:52:04.590
this is also an obstacle
that we see with people

00:52:04.590 --> 00:52:07.340
looking at Ranked Choice
Voting versus Approval Voting.

00:52:08.190 --> 00:52:11.560
But if you're filling out your ballot

00:52:11.560 --> 00:52:13.400
and you're looking at the results,

00:52:13.400 --> 00:52:16.010
and you don't see your voice
reflected in the results,

00:52:16.010 --> 00:52:17.500
something is wrong.

00:52:17.500 --> 00:52:21.940
You look at, and I see a lot
of third parties being told

00:52:21.940 --> 00:52:25.010
that Rank Choice voting
is the solution for them,

00:52:25.010 --> 00:52:26.420
it gives them their voice.

00:52:26.420 --> 00:52:29.950
You look at Maine, in
Maine, which was able to

00:52:29.950 --> 00:52:32.350
use Rank Choice Voting for
presidential elections,

00:52:32.350 --> 00:52:35.160
neither third party got over 2%.

00:52:35.160 --> 00:52:38.700
If Rank Choice Voting is
supposed to help third parties,

00:52:38.700 --> 00:52:39.630
where is it happening?

00:52:39.630 --> 00:52:41.320
You're looking at their opposites there,

00:52:41.320 --> 00:52:43.460
they're not getting over 5%.

00:52:43.460 --> 00:52:45.550
Whereas when we look at Approval Voting,

00:52:45.550 --> 00:52:48.530
time and again, like we
looked at the 2016 election,

00:52:48.530 --> 00:52:53.070
doing polling there Jill
Stein went from 1% to 9%.

00:52:53.070 --> 00:52:57.410
Gary Johnson went from 3% to 21%.

00:52:57.410 --> 00:52:59.230
You're not seeing any of those gains

00:52:59.230 --> 00:53:00.260
with Rank Choice Voting.

00:53:00.260 --> 00:53:02.870
So particularly, if you wanna
see your voice reflected

00:53:02.870 --> 00:53:04.800
regardless of what you put on your ballot,

00:53:04.800 --> 00:53:06.240
you need to use Approval Voting.

00:53:06.240 --> 00:53:08.240
That's what's gonna give you your voice.

00:53:09.920 --> 00:53:10.763
- Thank you.

00:53:12.140 --> 00:53:17.140
It's time for each of you to
give your closing statements.

00:53:17.710 --> 00:53:19.610
You each have three minutes.

00:53:19.610 --> 00:53:21.923
- Do you wanna give a Lee his rebuttal?

00:53:23.730 --> 00:53:25.700
- That question we just put on a table,

00:53:25.700 --> 00:53:30.060
and each of you got your
three minutes for that one.

00:53:30.060 --> 00:53:30.893
- [Aaron] All right.

00:53:30.893 --> 00:53:35.363
- I'll rule my rebuttal
into the closing statement.

00:53:36.232 --> 00:53:37.815
- All right, well, you're up.

00:53:37.815 --> 00:53:38.815
- All right.

00:53:42.797 --> 00:53:46.272
So, I didn't know that a
hundred million has been

00:53:46.272 --> 00:53:50.000
spent supporting Rank
Choice Voting efforts.

00:53:50.000 --> 00:53:53.500
That's new to me, but it suggest,

00:53:53.500 --> 00:53:55.552
to me that the funders who are looking

00:53:55.552 --> 00:53:58.500
at voting alternatives
are seeing something

00:53:58.500 --> 00:53:59.400
in Rank Choice Voting.

00:53:59.400 --> 00:54:01.780
I mean, these are folks
who are very careful

00:54:01.780 --> 00:54:06.150
with their money, and
do, from my experience,

00:54:06.150 --> 00:54:08.290
do run all the traps and try to figure out

00:54:08.290 --> 00:54:11.570
what things are effective and whatnot.

00:54:11.570 --> 00:54:13.270
And the fact that a
hundred million dollars

00:54:13.270 --> 00:54:15.810
has already been committed
to Rank Choice Voting,

00:54:15.810 --> 00:54:18.320
suggests that a lot of
people have independently

00:54:18.320 --> 00:54:20.550
come to the idea that Rank Choice Voting

00:54:20.550 --> 00:54:24.353
is a good system of voting.

00:54:25.520 --> 00:54:26.900
And again it's not, like,

00:54:26.900 --> 00:54:28.940
Approval Voting has been
around for a long time,

00:54:28.940 --> 00:54:32.200
Rank Choice voting has been
around for a long time,

00:54:32.200 --> 00:54:36.260
So this seems to me something notable.

00:54:36.260 --> 00:54:40.210
I mean Rank Choice Voting is
not gonna win every campaign,

00:54:40.210 --> 00:54:41.623
neither is Approval Voting.

00:54:42.460 --> 00:54:47.460
The women's suffrage movement
didn't win every campaign

00:54:47.470 --> 00:54:49.033
when it started out either.

00:54:51.548 --> 00:54:53.410
Do wanna address the idea,

00:54:53.410 --> 00:54:56.840
of the question of which is simpler?

00:54:56.840 --> 00:54:58.640
'Cause that was a question
that we were gonna

00:54:58.640 --> 00:55:00.340
discuss before.

00:55:00.340 --> 00:55:05.340
And I think that an argument
that I hear from Aaron is that

00:55:06.370 --> 00:55:09.483
Approval Voting is easy and cheap to do.

00:55:10.650 --> 00:55:12.820
So, when I hear that, it makes me think,

00:55:12.820 --> 00:55:14.190
well, okay, if it's easy and cheap,

00:55:14.190 --> 00:55:17.570
why hasn't anybody done it before?

00:55:17.570 --> 00:55:18.403
In fact, people have.

00:55:18.403 --> 00:55:20.690
There have been various
uses of Approval Voting,

00:55:20.690 --> 00:55:22.390
and there are a number of organizations

00:55:22.390 --> 00:55:23.310
that have adopted it,

00:55:23.310 --> 00:55:28.180
and almost invariably
devolves into Bullet voting,

00:55:28.180 --> 00:55:30.180
and they say, this doesn't really work.

00:55:30.180 --> 00:55:33.560
And like all voting
systems have trade offs,

00:55:33.560 --> 00:55:35.010
and there's complexities,

00:55:35.010 --> 00:55:37.672
and in some ways,

00:55:37.672 --> 00:55:40.120
if you want a super simple voting system,

00:55:40.120 --> 00:55:41.883
you can use Plurality voting,

00:55:45.750 --> 00:55:48.360
but the Approval Voting
hides its complexity,

00:55:48.360 --> 00:55:51.580
because the complexity is all
in the strategic calculations

00:55:51.580 --> 00:55:53.190
of whether or not people should approve

00:55:53.190 --> 00:55:55.543
of multiple candidates.

00:55:55.543 --> 00:55:56.820
Rank Choice Voting, yeah,

00:55:56.820 --> 00:55:58.640
t's a little bit more complicated upfront,

00:55:58.640 --> 00:56:00.570
but it's a very difficult system to game,

00:56:00.570 --> 00:56:04.300
and it really encourages
people to sincerely vote.

00:56:06.040 --> 00:56:09.440
Now, I hear Aaron saying, Oh, well,

00:56:09.440 --> 00:56:13.199
we know that approval works
better because we've run polls.

00:56:13.199 --> 00:56:16.460
A challenge for Approval Voting is that

00:56:16.460 --> 00:56:19.540
all Approval Voting have are these polls,

00:56:19.540 --> 00:56:23.900
because there's just not a
lot of real world examples.

00:56:23.900 --> 00:56:26.904
And again, I just as somebody,

00:56:26.904 --> 00:56:28.770
as a trained political scientist

00:56:28.770 --> 00:56:32.080
with a PhD in political science,
author of several books,

00:56:32.080 --> 00:56:34.330
somebody who really takes research

00:56:34.330 --> 00:56:37.780
and empirical analysis seriously,

00:56:37.780 --> 00:56:41.800
there are lots and lots of
studies of Rank Choice Voting

00:56:41.800 --> 00:56:44.900
with real data both in the
U.S and around the world.

00:56:44.900 --> 00:56:46.980
So, you can look at a stack of research

00:56:46.980 --> 00:56:48.840
and you can make your own conclusions.

00:56:48.840 --> 00:56:52.810
With Approval Voting, it's
largely mathematical theory,

00:56:52.810 --> 00:56:54.020
abstracted from reality.

00:56:54.020 --> 00:56:56.010
Now we have a few cities
that are getting using it

00:56:56.010 --> 00:56:59.303
for two winner elections,
not Single Winner elections.

00:57:02.623 --> 00:57:05.013
And it's all theoretical.

00:57:06.169 --> 00:57:07.810
I think the theory relies

00:57:07.810 --> 00:57:09.590
on a bunch of unrealistic assumptions

00:57:09.590 --> 00:57:11.650
about how politics actually works.

00:57:11.650 --> 00:57:14.130
I think the folks who put
a hundred million dollars

00:57:14.130 --> 00:57:17.810
into Rank Choice Voting
would probably agree again,

00:57:17.810 --> 00:57:21.730
these are folks who don't
spend their money Willy nilly.

00:57:21.730 --> 00:57:26.730
So, I think, with Rank Choice Voting,

00:57:26.758 --> 00:57:29.140
we don't have to guess.

00:57:29.140 --> 00:57:33.610
We know it works, and we know
that people are happy with it,

00:57:33.610 --> 00:57:35.300
and we know it encourages more

00:57:35.300 --> 00:57:38.010
positive compromise oriented campaigning.

00:57:38.010 --> 00:57:40.950
We can look around the world,
we can look in the U.S.

00:57:40.950 --> 00:57:44.140
And to me, that's a very compelling case.

00:57:44.140 --> 00:57:45.627
I've looked at the research,

00:57:45.627 --> 00:57:49.890
I've looked at it deeply,
and so have others.

00:57:49.890 --> 00:57:52.403
I mean, if you don't believe me,

00:57:52.403 --> 00:57:56.340
Barack Obama supporter
Ranked Choice voting,

00:57:56.340 --> 00:57:59.813
Elizabeth Warren supporter
of of Rank Choice Voting.

00:58:02.230 --> 00:58:07.230
There are a lot of folks
in Congress who support it.

00:58:08.030 --> 00:58:10.211
I have not heard any support

00:58:10.211 --> 00:58:13.023
at that level for Approval Voting.

00:58:14.920 --> 00:58:15.753
- Okay.

00:58:15.753 --> 00:58:16.586
Aaron?

00:58:19.010 --> 00:58:22.940
- So, Lee mentioned the funders,

00:58:22.940 --> 00:58:27.940
and so, I would really
ask the funders to think

00:58:29.490 --> 00:58:31.680
about where they're
putting their investments.

00:58:31.680 --> 00:58:33.840
When you look at a campaign
like Massachusetts,

00:58:33.840 --> 00:58:38.840
like, that has to hurt when
you are seeing a campaign

00:58:38.870 --> 00:58:43.833
that drops $30 million
and loses by 10 points.

00:58:44.870 --> 00:58:46.340
And I think the reason for that,

00:58:46.340 --> 00:58:48.340
one big reason for that is,

00:58:48.340 --> 00:58:51.500
people saw that this wasn't
a voting method for them.

00:58:51.500 --> 00:58:53.973
This was a voting method
for the establishment.

00:58:55.120 --> 00:58:58.980
What Rank Choice voting does,
is, if you like new voices,

00:58:58.980 --> 00:59:01.090
well, sure, go ahead.

00:59:01.090 --> 00:59:03.360
And if you wanna put them as first,

00:59:03.360 --> 00:59:06.110
but those are just gonna
get transferred over

00:59:06.110 --> 00:59:09.370
to other candidates and your
voice is not gonna get heard.

00:59:09.370 --> 00:59:10.600
And you see this explicitly.

00:59:10.600 --> 00:59:11.976
Like I mentioned, Maine,

00:59:11.976 --> 00:59:16.976
Maine was Ranked Choice Voting
opportunity to show itself.

00:59:17.210 --> 00:59:22.210
If Ranked Choice Voting allowed
you to express your voice

00:59:22.670 --> 00:59:25.200
in a meaningful way, and
actually get it heard,

00:59:25.200 --> 00:59:26.360
Maine would have shown that

00:59:26.360 --> 00:59:30.193
But Rank Choice Voting
is ignoring your voice.

00:59:34.420 --> 00:59:37.840
If Rank Choice voting would
have worked for new voices,

00:59:37.840 --> 00:59:41.090
you would have seen more support
for third parties in Maine,

00:59:41.090 --> 00:59:44.383
a state that's known
for independent voices.

00:59:47.450 --> 00:59:48.630
So, when we're looking at this,

00:59:48.630 --> 00:59:50.730
again we have to go back and think

00:59:50.730 --> 00:59:53.770
what are the core components that we need

00:59:53.770 --> 00:59:54.603
in a voting method?

00:59:54.603 --> 00:59:57.510
We need a voting method
that gets us clear winners.

00:59:57.510 --> 01:00:00.373
We have real data right now in St. Louis.

01:00:02.040 --> 01:00:06.720
We had previously a mayor
who won with a fraction

01:00:06.720 --> 01:00:11.633
of the vote and vote splitting
among the black community.

01:00:12.560 --> 01:00:16.030
It was so apparent after
Proposition D passed

01:00:16.030 --> 01:00:18.290
by over 60% that the former mayor

01:00:18.290 --> 01:00:20.330
wasn't gonna get away with it anymore.

01:00:20.330 --> 01:00:22.330
She decided not to run as an incumbent.

01:00:22.330 --> 01:00:24.980
She decided not to run
because she saw the writing

01:00:24.980 --> 01:00:28.860
on the wall that the people's
voice was gonna be heard,

01:00:28.860 --> 01:00:30.280
and it was heard.

01:00:30.280 --> 01:00:34.730
That the candidate that previously lost,

01:00:34.730 --> 01:00:39.730
now was leading with well over 50%

01:00:39.750 --> 01:00:43.190
in that first round,
going into the runoff.

01:00:43.190 --> 01:00:45.100
So, we have real-world data.

01:00:45.100 --> 01:00:47.880
And the reason that we do
polling alongside of this,

01:00:47.880 --> 01:00:52.820
is to see whether the actual best winner

01:00:52.820 --> 01:00:53.890
was the one who won.

01:00:53.890 --> 01:00:57.690
We can't just at an election and say,

01:00:57.690 --> 01:01:00.043
Oh, well, there, there was a result there,

01:01:00.043 --> 01:01:02.010
a voting method succeeded.

01:01:02.010 --> 01:01:04.120
You have to take data alongside that

01:01:04.120 --> 01:01:07.440
to see whether the
winner was a good winner.

01:01:07.440 --> 01:01:09.790
And you don't know that
unless you collect the data.

01:01:09.790 --> 01:01:10.790
That's what we do.

01:01:10.790 --> 01:01:15.300
Otherwise, you're just
hypothesizing or speculating

01:01:16.740 --> 01:01:18.440
whether your voting method work.

01:01:18.440 --> 01:01:19.273
You can't do that.

01:01:19.273 --> 01:01:21.110
You actually have to collect data

01:01:21.110 --> 01:01:23.970
to be able to tell whether your
voting method was effective.

01:01:23.970 --> 01:01:25.890
And that's what we do.

01:01:25.890 --> 01:01:30.180
And so, we have a solution
in front of us right now

01:01:30.180 --> 01:01:31.660
that is easy to implement,

01:01:31.660 --> 01:01:33.440
and it's easy to understand.

01:01:33.440 --> 01:01:36.500
You have a drastically better alternative

01:01:36.500 --> 01:01:37.333
than you've been told.

01:01:37.333 --> 01:01:39.390
You don't have to wait for permission.

01:01:39.390 --> 01:01:41.860
They're not gonna give
you permission anyway.

01:01:41.860 --> 01:01:43.000
You have to demand it.

01:01:43.000 --> 01:01:44.860
So I ask that you join us

01:01:44.860 --> 01:01:47.210
in a room to spread Approval Voting.

01:01:47.210 --> 01:01:50.170
Our democracy needs you now,.

01:01:50.170 --> 01:01:51.003
Thank you.

01:01:52.380 --> 01:01:53.800
- Thank you.

01:01:53.800 --> 01:01:56.700
I think that Andrew
might have some questions

01:01:56.700 --> 01:01:57.830
from the audience.

01:01:57.830 --> 01:01:59.810
If not, I have a question of my own,

01:01:59.810 --> 01:02:02.270
and then I have a question
that someone sent to me

01:02:02.270 --> 01:02:04.100
earlier in the week

01:02:04.100 --> 01:02:06.280
- Or so do I ever have questions?

01:02:06.280 --> 01:02:08.760
There have been 112 questions submitted

01:02:08.760 --> 01:02:10.020
in the chat below us.

01:02:10.020 --> 01:02:11.610
- Holly guacamole!

01:02:11.610 --> 01:02:13.415
- There is no way that I will be able

01:02:13.415 --> 01:02:15.730
to get through all of them.

01:02:15.730 --> 01:02:19.840
I have tried to identify a
handful that had common themes

01:02:19.840 --> 01:02:22.150
throughout, but I'll let you go firstly.

01:02:22.150 --> 01:02:24.370
So, you said you had
one on top of your head,

01:02:24.370 --> 01:02:28.140
and we want to hear your thoughts as well.

01:02:28.140 --> 01:02:28.973
- Yeah.

01:02:28.973 --> 01:02:31.130
So, my question is for Andrew.

01:02:31.130 --> 01:02:32.710
A couple of times you...

01:02:32.710 --> 01:02:33.810
Not Andrew, I'm sorry.

01:02:33.810 --> 01:02:38.360
Aaron, and Aaron, a couple
of times you've referred to

01:02:38.360 --> 01:02:43.360
Maine showing that the
third party candidates,

01:02:44.748 --> 01:02:47.110
that didn't really work for them.

01:02:47.110 --> 01:02:51.200
So can you walk us through
why Maine was a failure

01:02:51.200 --> 01:02:52.573
from your perspective?

01:02:53.810 --> 01:02:58.160
- Yeah, so, in Maine, neither
the Green party candidate

01:02:58.160 --> 01:03:02.690
nor the Libertarian party
candidate got more than 2%.

01:03:03.613 --> 01:03:04.750
And so, we have to ask ourselves, like,

01:03:04.750 --> 01:03:07.640
did they actually have
more support than 2%?

01:03:08.630 --> 01:03:10.453
And surely they did.

01:03:12.055 --> 01:03:15.000
And the reason that Rank
Choice Voting fails to show

01:03:15.000 --> 01:03:17.750
that support is because it just ignores,

01:03:17.750 --> 01:03:20.110
it thoroughly ignores data.

01:03:20.110 --> 01:03:24.470
So, you look at all the
information that people put down

01:03:24.470 --> 01:03:25.862
on their ballot.

01:03:25.862 --> 01:03:30.090
So, if a candidate isn't
eliminated into a later round,

01:03:30.090 --> 01:03:33.450
all the lower rankings for those ballots,

01:03:33.450 --> 01:03:35.930
never even acknowledged
by the voting method.

01:03:35.930 --> 01:03:38.600
And so, that never is reflected at all

01:03:38.600 --> 01:03:41.770
in terms of support for
those third party candidates.

01:03:41.770 --> 01:03:44.830
Whereas with Approval Voting,
doesn't ignore any data.

01:03:44.830 --> 01:03:46.300
It uses all of the information.

01:03:46.300 --> 01:03:48.790
So, if you have a voice
that you wanna give,

01:03:48.790 --> 01:03:50.450
Approval Voting picks up on that,

01:03:50.450 --> 01:03:52.180
cause it doesn't ignore anything.

01:03:52.180 --> 01:03:54.750
And that works out
excellently for third parties,

01:03:54.750 --> 01:03:57.863
independents, because their
voice is always heard.

01:03:59.020 --> 01:04:00.040
- Okay.

01:04:00.040 --> 01:04:01.021
Thank you.

01:04:01.021 --> 01:04:03.683
Andrew, did you wanna
start with your questions

01:04:03.683 --> 01:04:04.800
from the audience.

01:04:04.800 --> 01:04:07.130
- So, we have about 10 minutes left

01:04:07.130 --> 01:04:08.700
in what was scheduled.

01:04:08.700 --> 01:04:10.530
I'm all right with going a
little bit over with that,

01:04:10.530 --> 01:04:12.700
if you are, but not too far much further.

01:04:12.700 --> 01:04:14.720
So, I have one question targeted

01:04:14.720 --> 01:04:16.170
to each of you respectively,

01:04:16.170 --> 01:04:17.500
and then a question for both

01:04:17.500 --> 01:04:19.100
of you to answer at the end.

01:04:19.100 --> 01:04:21.470
So Lee, I'll start with you.

01:04:21.470 --> 01:04:24.460
Several audience members
have questioned just how well

01:04:24.460 --> 01:04:26.510
Rank Choice Voting has
actually worked in Ireland

01:04:26.510 --> 01:04:27.343
or Australia.

01:04:27.343 --> 01:04:29.980
They've asked whether it's
particularly popular there,

01:04:29.980 --> 01:04:31.780
or whether it has succeeded

01:04:31.780 --> 01:04:33.780
in developing strong third parties.

01:04:33.780 --> 01:04:38.230
One of them pointed out that
Australia has fewer independent

01:04:38.230 --> 01:04:40.750
or minor party seats than say Canada,

01:04:40.750 --> 01:04:43.830
even though Canada still
use first past the post.

01:04:43.830 --> 01:04:45.260
So, do you have a response to that?

01:04:45.260 --> 01:04:48.520
And then how important is the
attraction of third parties

01:04:48.520 --> 01:04:50.770
as a criteria in evaluating these systems

01:04:50.770 --> 01:04:52.580
compared to other things?

01:04:52.580 --> 01:04:54.370
- Well, look, if you want third parties,

01:04:54.370 --> 01:04:56.230
you have to get rid of
Single Winner elections.

01:04:56.230 --> 01:04:58.039
I mean, it's just as simple as that.

01:04:58.039 --> 01:05:01.780
Aaron accuses me of
conflating RCV with STV,

01:05:01.780 --> 01:05:04.290
which is the multi winner
form of Rank Choice Voting.

01:05:04.290 --> 01:05:06.411
I see it as a family of systems.

01:05:06.411 --> 01:05:08.855
We're debating the Single Winner form

01:05:08.855 --> 01:05:12.800
of Ranked Choice Voting, but
I think we have to consider

01:05:12.800 --> 01:05:17.211
the whole family of Rank
Choice Voting system.

01:05:17.211 --> 01:05:20.420
Ireland has three to five parties roughly,

01:05:20.420 --> 01:05:23.020
Ireland does use the multi winner forms.

01:05:23.020 --> 01:05:26.120
Australia use the Single
Winner form for its house,

01:05:26.120 --> 01:05:28.660
which tends to have two
parties use the multi winner

01:05:28.660 --> 01:05:30.840
for it's Senate, which
tends to have more parties.

01:05:30.840 --> 01:05:33.180
Canada has a high degree of federalism,

01:05:33.180 --> 01:05:35.240
which allows for multiple parties

01:05:35.240 --> 01:05:37.913
in the strong Quebec block.

01:05:38.830 --> 01:05:43.640
Look, if you had...

01:05:44.804 --> 01:05:47.930
I mean, Aaron's talking
about the Green party

01:05:47.930 --> 01:05:50.810
in presidential elections,

01:05:50.810 --> 01:05:53.160
and in a year in which
the stakes were very high,

01:05:53.160 --> 01:05:55.660
there was just not a lot of
attention on third party.

01:05:55.660 --> 01:05:58.069
And I mean, maybe the
Green party would have

01:05:58.069 --> 01:06:00.080
gotten 4% of 2%.

01:06:00.080 --> 01:06:03.573
I mean, this is kind of
picking on the edges.

01:06:05.120 --> 01:06:08.500
So, yeah, yeah, yeah.

01:06:08.500 --> 01:06:10.684
Single one Ranked Choice
Voting is not gonna generate

01:06:10.684 --> 01:06:13.790
a ton of support for third parties,

01:06:13.790 --> 01:06:16.490
but neither is Approval Voting either.

01:06:16.490 --> 01:06:18.380
You have to move past
the Single Winner format

01:06:18.380 --> 01:06:21.810
if you really want multiple parties.

01:06:21.810 --> 01:06:25.100
And I mean, I'm honestly confused
when Aaron's talking about

01:06:25.100 --> 01:06:28.720
the establishment supporting
Rank Choice Voting

01:06:28.720 --> 01:06:30.810
because, if he had followed the campaign

01:06:30.810 --> 01:06:33.520
at all in Maine, he knew
that the establishment there

01:06:33.520 --> 01:06:36.400
was incredibly against Rank Choice Voting,

01:06:36.400 --> 01:06:41.350
and it took two initiatives
in order to get it passed,

01:06:41.350 --> 01:06:44.290
because the established
firm tried to shut it down.

01:06:44.290 --> 01:06:48.463
So, that part is confusing
to me, to confess.

01:06:50.630 --> 01:06:52.110
- Aaron, my next question for you,

01:06:52.110 --> 01:06:54.470
you can squeeze in a little
response to that if you want,

01:06:54.470 --> 01:06:57.061
just 'cause I know we're trying to allow

01:06:57.061 --> 01:06:59.360
equitable response times.

01:06:59.360 --> 01:07:03.470
The Approval Voting specific
question that I had was,

01:07:03.470 --> 01:07:06.170
is there any hazard regarding the variance

01:07:06.170 --> 01:07:08.450
from person to person
in what approval means?

01:07:08.450 --> 01:07:11.470
Or the different thresholds
that some people would express

01:07:11.470 --> 01:07:13.521
that they approve of a
candidate in their positions,

01:07:13.521 --> 01:07:15.533
whereas Rank Choice Voting
kind of gets around that,

01:07:15.533 --> 01:07:17.820
because were only expressing
relative preferences

01:07:17.820 --> 01:07:19.080
rather than an absolute one.

01:07:19.080 --> 01:07:23.192
So, how much is that ambiguity
in what approval signifies

01:07:23.192 --> 01:07:26.823
a problem to the accuracy
of approval voting results?

01:07:29.740 --> 01:07:33.530
- That threshold per individual may vary.

01:07:33.530 --> 01:07:36.530
But despite that, when we were looking at

01:07:36.530 --> 01:07:40.540
honest assessments as the
electorate as a whole,

01:07:40.540 --> 01:07:42.800
we're still seeing that
strong winner getting elected,

01:07:42.800 --> 01:07:45.750
and ultimately that's what matters.

01:07:45.750 --> 01:07:50.600
I'm a little put off by
these responses about

01:07:51.520 --> 01:07:55.300
it not mattering about
being able to capture

01:07:55.300 --> 01:07:57.980
third-parties or independent support.

01:07:57.980 --> 01:08:02.200
These are new voices, and also we...

01:08:03.650 --> 01:08:05.640
But this is basically arguing for is that

01:08:05.640 --> 01:08:06.980
we ignore people's voice.

01:08:06.980 --> 01:08:09.730
And I don't think that's just at all.

01:08:09.730 --> 01:08:11.770
So, like when we look at
the Democratic primary

01:08:11.770 --> 01:08:14.290
for instance, I pointed out
that Yang, for instance,

01:08:14.290 --> 01:08:17.100
under both Rank Choice
voting and Plurality voting

01:08:17.100 --> 01:08:18.710
was getting under 10%.

01:08:18.710 --> 01:08:21.440
Are we saying that it's
okay for new voices

01:08:21.440 --> 01:08:24.490
to get marginalized, because
that's what's happening

01:08:24.490 --> 01:08:25.810
under Plurality voting,

01:08:25.810 --> 01:08:28.090
and that's what's continuing to happen

01:08:28.090 --> 01:08:29.690
under Rank Choice Voting.

01:08:29.690 --> 01:08:33.700
Whereas the Approval Voting
gives those candidates support

01:08:33.700 --> 01:08:35.850
so that they can't be marginalized

01:08:35.850 --> 01:08:37.020
when they bring in new voices.

01:08:37.020 --> 01:08:40.340
I don't think this kind
of unfair marginalization

01:08:40.340 --> 01:08:41.880
is what we want in a voting method.

01:08:41.880 --> 01:08:44.203
And again, that's why
we want Approval Voting,

01:08:44.203 --> 01:08:47.410
because we wanna be able
to pick up on those voices.

01:08:47.410 --> 01:08:48.243
- Come on.

01:08:48.243 --> 01:08:50.327
That's a bad faith
argument and you know it.

01:08:51.183 --> 01:08:52.608
- I mean, just look at the data.

01:08:52.608 --> 01:08:53.930
- Are we gonna argue about new voices?

01:08:53.930 --> 01:08:54.763
Come on.

01:08:54.763 --> 01:08:56.900
You know it's a bad faith arguments.

01:08:56.900 --> 01:08:57.770
- Look at the data.

01:08:57.770 --> 01:09:02.770
I mean, and Maine, these other candidates

01:09:03.060 --> 01:09:04.000
are getting a pittance of their support

01:09:04.000 --> 01:09:07.020
- You calling Green Party a new voice?

01:09:07.020 --> 01:09:10.850
I mean, look, I mean, if the
Green party had more support

01:09:10.850 --> 01:09:12.460
we'd see it in polls.

01:09:12.460 --> 01:09:15.146
We don't need voting
methods and Andrew Yang,

01:09:15.146 --> 01:09:17.440
I mean, look, Andrew Yang
has not been marginalized.

01:09:17.440 --> 01:09:19.000
He's running for mayor of New York city,

01:09:19.000 --> 01:09:21.210
he has a huge Yang gang.

01:09:21.210 --> 01:09:22.990
he wasn't gonna win the
democratic primary anyway,

01:09:22.990 --> 01:09:24.630
but he got a ton of attention

01:09:24.630 --> 01:09:27.383
and he really did elevate some new ideas.

01:09:29.450 --> 01:09:32.580
- I have a hard time
understanding how these voices

01:09:32.580 --> 01:09:35.770
would have been picked up in polls.

01:09:35.770 --> 01:09:37.670
They use Plurality voting.

01:09:37.670 --> 01:09:41.230
When you use a terrible
measurement instrument,

01:09:41.230 --> 01:09:43.109
of course, it's not gonna be picked up.

01:09:43.109 --> 01:09:46.310
- Aaron, run all the Approval
Voting polls you want,

01:09:46.310 --> 01:09:47.690
I mean, I don't care.

01:09:47.690 --> 01:09:48.740
I mean, that doesn't matter.

01:09:48.740 --> 01:09:49.640
I mean, it's a good question,

01:09:49.640 --> 01:09:51.353
but when people are
actually having to vote,

01:09:51.353 --> 01:09:53.333
they have to vote strategically.

01:09:54.400 --> 01:09:55.733
So like, no...

01:09:57.060 --> 01:09:57.900
I mean I'm not trying to...

01:09:57.900 --> 01:09:58.950
I want more voices.

01:09:58.950 --> 01:10:01.600
I'm the guy whose argument
for multi-party democracy.

01:10:03.670 --> 01:10:05.490
I mean, I think we're rushing up here,

01:10:05.490 --> 01:10:06.580
we're rushing up against the limits

01:10:06.580 --> 01:10:08.140
of the Single Winner system.

01:10:08.140 --> 01:10:11.010
That if you really wanted more
voices, you should join me

01:10:11.010 --> 01:10:13.160
in supporting proportional representation.

01:10:13.160 --> 01:10:16.800
I mean, if that's what's
really important to you, Aaron,

01:10:16.800 --> 01:10:19.270
and maybe it is,
proportional representation

01:10:19.270 --> 01:10:21.180
is gonna bring in a lot more new voices.

01:10:21.180 --> 01:10:22.793
So will you join me in that?

01:10:23.951 --> 01:10:26.950
- I'm a supporter of
proportional representation,

01:10:26.950 --> 01:10:29.330
and if that's what this debate was about,

01:10:29.330 --> 01:10:30.520
I would have talked about it more,

01:10:30.520 --> 01:10:34.610
but this debate is
about addressing offices

01:10:34.610 --> 01:10:36.973
that are inherently a Single Winner.

01:10:38.040 --> 01:10:40.310
And it's important
that, for those offices,

01:10:40.310 --> 01:10:43.550
that we'd make sure that we
get strong people elected.

01:10:43.550 --> 01:10:47.140
And then when people have voices
that are bringing new ideas

01:10:47.140 --> 01:10:49.020
to the table, that we don't ignore them,

01:10:49.020 --> 01:10:51.140
because that's what's happening
under Plurality voting,

01:10:51.140 --> 01:10:53.240
that's what's happening
under Ranked Choice Voting.

01:10:53.240 --> 01:10:55.490
And we don't have to look
at hypotheticals anymore.

01:10:55.490 --> 01:11:00.113
You can't argue that people
are gonna constantly use

01:11:01.780 --> 01:11:05.970
tactical strategies in a way
that nullifies the election,

01:11:05.970 --> 01:11:08.800
when we have firsthand
knowledge of actual elections

01:11:08.800 --> 01:11:10.770
when that's not coming to fruition.

01:11:10.770 --> 01:11:12.330
And as Approval Voting gets implemented

01:11:12.330 --> 01:11:13.740
in more and more places,

01:11:13.740 --> 01:11:15.760
we're gonna see even more
and more examples of that.

01:11:15.760 --> 01:11:17.292
And it's gonna be harder

01:11:17.292 --> 01:11:19.591
to put out those bad faith arguments.

01:11:19.591 --> 01:11:20.900
- All right, well, I'm looking forward

01:11:20.900 --> 01:11:23.380
to seeing Approval Voting implemented

01:11:23.380 --> 01:11:27.470
in a place with a Single Winner.

01:11:27.470 --> 01:11:30.010
I mean, I find it interesting that,

01:11:30.010 --> 01:11:31.330
although we cite St. Louis.

01:11:31.330 --> 01:11:33.600
Actually St. Louis has a runoff.

01:11:33.600 --> 01:11:35.750
So, it's sort of like,

01:11:35.750 --> 01:11:38.060
I mean RCV is also called
instant runoff voting.

01:11:38.060 --> 01:11:40.150
If Approval Voting works so well,

01:11:40.150 --> 01:11:42.470
then why not just have it
elect the mayor directly?

01:11:42.470 --> 01:11:45.123
Why do you need a runoff election?

01:11:46.290 --> 01:11:49.163
- The reason in St. Louis
that we use a runoff is

01:11:49.163 --> 01:11:51.470
because in some cases there are parameters

01:11:51.470 --> 01:11:56.470
about how a state may
interpret what a majority is,

01:11:56.700 --> 01:11:58.033
but I think it's important to recognize

01:11:58.033 --> 01:12:01.480
the new voting method
can guarantee a majority

01:12:01.480 --> 01:12:03.720
by merely throwing out candidates

01:12:03.720 --> 01:12:07.230
until there are two or three
just is contriving a majority.

01:12:07.230 --> 01:12:10.160
So, in those cases, we're
doing it just to satisfy

01:12:10.160 --> 01:12:13.230
a state's interpretation of majority.

01:12:13.230 --> 01:12:15.710
And fortunately approved
learning is so flexible

01:12:15.710 --> 01:12:17.690
that it is able to work with a lot

01:12:17.690 --> 01:12:20.090
of different circumstances,
including the one in St. Louis.

01:12:20.090 --> 01:12:24.580
But I don't think anyone
would argue in good faith

01:12:24.580 --> 01:12:27.150
that traditional runoff system is

01:12:27.150 --> 01:12:29.280
not a Single Winner method,

01:12:29.280 --> 01:12:31.520
whether you're using it
with Plurality voting,

01:12:31.520 --> 01:12:33.723
or if you're using it
with Approval Voting.

01:12:34.760 --> 01:12:37.880
- But then why not just have
it for the final result?

01:12:37.880 --> 01:12:40.313
I mean, anyway, that's
an academic argument.

01:12:42.370 --> 01:12:46.480
- I am glad that the discussion
has become almost as lively

01:12:46.480 --> 01:12:49.250
as the comment section beneath us.

01:12:49.250 --> 01:12:53.840
There is one more question
I think we have time for,

01:12:53.840 --> 01:12:55.300
which is maybe a little bit less,

01:12:55.300 --> 01:12:58.020
where you might not necessarily disagree.

01:12:58.020 --> 01:13:00.100
There's a lot of comments coming in

01:13:00.100 --> 01:13:02.550
pointing out that there are
many other voting methods,

01:13:02.550 --> 01:13:05.630
besides those, even within
the Single Winner limitations,

01:13:05.630 --> 01:13:07.750
besides those we've endorsed today,

01:13:07.750 --> 01:13:10.540
which is kind of ironic given
that escaping this doom loop

01:13:10.540 --> 01:13:12.110
of only two options to choose from

01:13:12.110 --> 01:13:14.700
is one of the things
we're aiming to achieve.

01:13:14.700 --> 01:13:18.010
Star voting for example,
or ranking in some way.

01:13:18.010 --> 01:13:20.480
So, what I'll ask you to close is,

01:13:20.480 --> 01:13:23.149
using your voting method
of choice, tell us what

01:13:23.149 --> 01:13:25.920
either your next favorite
method or methods are.

01:13:25.920 --> 01:13:29.440
So, Lee what's your second
favorite voting method,

01:13:29.440 --> 01:13:30.273
What's your third choice?

01:13:30.273 --> 01:13:33.101
And then Aaron, of what other
voting methods do you approve

01:13:33.101 --> 01:13:35.493
as improvements over the status quo?

01:13:38.350 --> 01:13:41.361
- Well, I mean, when it comes
to Single Winner elections,

01:13:41.361 --> 01:13:43.866
I would say either either
Ranked Choice Voting

01:13:43.866 --> 01:13:47.200
or a two round election, runoff election.

01:13:47.200 --> 01:13:50.110
When it comes to portion
representation I think we,

01:13:50.110 --> 01:13:54.280
in addition to STV, we
could consider openness PR,

01:13:54.280 --> 01:13:56.150
we can consider a mixed member proportion,

01:13:56.150 --> 01:13:59.051
the New Zealand, German system,

01:13:59.051 --> 01:14:03.363
but I feel like there's this desire among

01:14:03.363 --> 01:14:06.470
voting elections nerds,
and it's a fun thing to do

01:14:06.470 --> 01:14:08.623
to come up with some new voting system.

01:14:09.889 --> 01:14:13.332
But yeah, the reality
is that we've been...

01:14:13.332 --> 01:14:15.220
this is not a new debate.

01:14:15.220 --> 01:14:19.390
It's 150, 170 year old debate.

01:14:19.390 --> 01:14:21.960
And a lot of these
voting methods have been

01:14:21.960 --> 01:14:26.960
debated and tried, and
there's a reason why

01:14:27.800 --> 01:14:32.800
Rank Choice voting is a
very widely used method.

01:14:33.540 --> 01:14:36.680
And all of these other
families of voting methods

01:14:36.680 --> 01:14:40.410
are not used as because Rank Choice Voting

01:14:40.410 --> 01:14:42.013
works pretty well.

01:14:42.850 --> 01:14:44.623
And that's as simple as that.

01:14:48.130 --> 01:14:50.543
- In terms of other voting methods,

01:14:50.543 --> 01:14:55.290
I would say Score voting is
probably a strong runner up

01:14:55.290 --> 01:14:56.363
to Approval Voting.

01:14:57.580 --> 01:15:02.340
With Approval Voting, you're
looking at really kind of

01:15:03.820 --> 01:15:07.020
rare isolated cases where
like Approval Voting

01:15:07.020 --> 01:15:09.473
would not get the same
winner as Score voting,

01:15:10.600 --> 01:15:13.813
maybe fewer than one in a
hundred or something like that.

01:15:15.450 --> 01:15:16.770
But with Approval Voting,

01:15:16.770 --> 01:15:21.210
you're getting so much for so little

01:15:21.210 --> 01:15:25.600
in terms of complexity
costs and actual costs,

01:15:25.600 --> 01:15:28.600
that you're getting a real
bargain with the Approval voting,

01:15:29.652 --> 01:15:34.242
and looking at and comparing
with the alternatives now,

01:15:34.242 --> 01:15:36.690
that you're getting better performance

01:15:36.690 --> 01:15:39.422
than some of these alternatives
that are being proposed,

01:15:39.422 --> 01:15:43.670
and you're doing it while not having

01:15:43.670 --> 01:15:45.430
the complexity associated with it,

01:15:45.430 --> 01:15:48.800
and not having to have
the the extra expenses

01:15:48.800 --> 01:15:50.100
associated with it either.

01:15:53.200 --> 01:15:54.033
- All right.

01:15:54.910 --> 01:15:57.200
Then on that note, it is 4:45,

01:15:57.200 --> 01:16:00.140
which is the advertised
close of this event.

01:16:00.140 --> 01:16:01.130
Thank you all for coming,

01:16:01.130 --> 01:16:04.540
both participants and the attendees.

01:16:04.540 --> 01:16:06.500
We will post the recording afterwards

01:16:06.500 --> 01:16:09.350
on the Yale Effective
Altruism social media

01:16:09.350 --> 01:16:10.610
among other places.

01:16:10.610 --> 01:16:13.121
So, you will be able to
continue the conversation

01:16:13.121 --> 01:16:16.333
amongst yourselves, and thank you

01:16:19.646 --> 01:16:20.479
for what I think has been
a productive exchange.

01:16:20.479 --> 01:16:21.740
- Thank you.

01:16:21.740 --> 01:16:23.939
Thanks Andrew, thanks Lee, thanks Aaron.

01:16:23.939 --> 01:16:24.772
- Thank you.

